      passage: "Packaging Machine Belt Alerts<br><br>A factory tested a packaging machine by running it several times under the same operating settings. During each test run, a motion sensor recorded each short pause in the movement of the package belt. These pauses were called belt alerts. A belt alert was counted only if the belt stopped moving for at least 1 s.<br><br>For each run, technicians recorded the total run duration, the total number of belt alerts, and the greatest number of belt alerts recorded during any 6 min interval of that run. The results for 9 test runs are shown in Table 1.<br><br>Run E was selected for closer study because its total number of belt alerts was near the middle of the set. For Run E, technicians divided the full run into equal 6 min intervals and counted the number of belt alerts in each interval. These results are shown in Figure 1.<br><br>For the same Run E alerts, technicians also recorded belt speed at the moment just before each alert began. The alerts were grouped by belt speed range, as shown in Figure 2. The belt speed groups include all 46 alerts from Run E.<br><br>All test runs used the same packaging machine, package size, belt material, and sensor location. The package belt speed setting was also kept the same for all runs."
