      passage: "<b>Signals from the Street</b><br><br>While searching a campus archive portal for photographs of old storefronts, I found a profile of Leon Mercer, a retired transit worker <u>which had recorded</u><b>[1]</b> neighborhood voices and street sounds for more than thirty years. Mercer carried a small cassette recorder during walks, community meetings, and breaks between bus routes. Although his collection contained hundreds of tapes, few people beyond his family knew it existed.<br><br>Mercer had driven buses, repaired fare boxes, and announced arrivals at a neighborhood terminal. A longtime resident, he was <u>personally familiar with the neighborhood's local streets, routes, and gathering places</u><b>[2]</b>. At home, he labeled each tape with a date, intersection, and brief description but rarely played the recordings for an audience. After his relatives donated the boxes to a community archive, a public radio producer used several excerpts in a documentary. <u>Meanwhile</u><b>[3]</b>, schools requested listening copies, and the archive began hosting crowded evening programs.<br><br>The documentary transformed Mercer's private collection into a source that teachers and residents could use to examine neighborhood change. <u>It was broadcast on three stations during the same weekend.</u><b>[4]</b> One recording<u> <i>Saturday at Linden Market</i></u><b>[5]</b> contains <u>sounds in the form of noises from a vendor's call, a bus brake, and children chanting a game rhyme</u><b>[6]</b>. Near the end, a shopkeeper briefly describes a building that had already been demolished when Mercer made the tape. [A] I could identify the separate sounds. [B] I could not determine whether the interview and the market activity had been recorded at exactly the same place. [C] An archive time log identifies the speakers and locations Mercer noted during the recording.<b>[7]</b> <u>That uncertainty made the recording worth hearing again because each replay suggested a different connection among its details.</u><b>[8]</b> [D]<br><br><u>Mercer was calmly pleased that people had finally noticed his work.</u><b>[9]</b> The documentary explains that he valued the recordings because they preserved useful details, not because they eventually attracted attention. Leaving the media lab, I crossed a street I had walked many times before. A delivery cart rattled over a metal grate, a driver called through an open bus door, and someone swept glass from a sidewalk. The sounds no longer seemed like background noise alone; they also seemed capable of recording where and when a community had lived."
