      practiceYear: 1,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Word Choice and Usage",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>A Film Assembled from Fragments</b><br><br>Many silent films circulated as several physical prints rather than one permanent master copy. The film stock could shrink, tear, burn, or decompose, and distributors sometimes shortened a movie for a particular audience. As a result, one archive might hold an opening sequence while another preserves scenes missing from that copy.<br><br>A restoration begins when archivists <u>stumble upon</u><b>[1]</b> surviving prints and inspect their condition. Each print is scanned frame by frame<u>, software can then align repeated images and expose differences in length or sequence.</u><b>[2]</b> <u>Language, distributor marks, and censorship records can help explain why a particular print contains altered intertitles or missing scenes.</u><b>[3]</b> Before comparison, each print receives <u>their</u><b>[4]</b> own record of damage, repairs, and provenance.<br><br>No surviving copy is automatically treated as complete. Archivists compare scene order, camera action, intertitles, and continuity between shots. <u>Nevertheless,</u><b>[5]</b> a brief passage preserved in one country may fill a gap in a longer print stored elsewhere. When several versions disagree, researchers <u>have consulted</u><b>[6]</b> scripts, reviews, production records, and censorship files <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[7]</b> evaluate the alternatives.<br><br>Digital tools can stabilize jitter, correct speed, and balance large changes in brightness. A series of automated corrections <u>are</u><b>[8]</b> reviewed by a human operator so that scratches or camera movement are not mistaken for defects to erase. Missing frames may be represented by a brief title or a documented still image rather than newly invented action.<br><br>[A] Intertitles present another reconstruction problem. [B] <u>A translated title may preserve the meaning of lost text while differing in typography, phrasing, or length.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] Restorers compare surviving versions and explain substantial choices in accompanying notes. [D] A musical score may also be commissioned or reconstructed, but it is identified separately from the photographed image.<br><br>The completed restoration is not a claim that damage and loss never occurred. It is a documented arrangement of the strongest surviving evidence. By allowing viewers to see a coherent film while preserving a record of uncertainty, the restoration makes both the original work and the history of its survival available for study.<b>[10]</b>"
