      passage: "<b>The Reliable Drawers</b><br><br>In many modern offices, rows of handwritten index cards seem outdated<u>: a judgment that overlooks their independence from digital systems</u><b>[1]</b>. Card drawers occupy valuable space, and searching them requires more physical effort than entering a name into a computer. <u>Paper cards require no password, battery, server, or network connection.</u><b>[2]</b><br><br>One hospital records department had preserved such an index even after most patient files became digital. Each card listed a patient number, a date range, and codes directing staff to related folders. The index's value depended on <u>it's cards cross-reference system, which connected each patients current number to earlier records</u><b>[3]</b>. Records supervisor Dana Ruiz insisted that new locations be added to the cards whenever older charts were transferred. <u>After updating the index at the end of each workday for more than twelve years, the drawers became familiar to Ruiz and her staff.</u><b>[4]</b> The cards contained <u>references that referred back to and cross-referenced other cards</u><b>[5]</b>. The series of handwritten cards in the central drawers <u>were</u><b>[6]</b> checked against the digital catalog each month. [A] The hospital had adopted electronic files in stages. [B] Staff continued updating both systems during the transition. [C] Ruiz argued that a backup used only during outages would still fail if its references were outdated. <u>For that reason, the department retained the paper index as a backup in case the digital catalog became unavailable.</u><b>[7]</b> [D]<br><br>That possibility became real when a regional network failure interrupted access to recent and archived records. Physicians needed allergy lists, medication histories, and reports from earlier admissions, but ordinary electronic searches returned error messages. For several hours, staff could not open the digital catalog from any workstation.<br><br>Records employees consulted the cards, retrieved boxed charts from storage rooms, and relayed verified details to clinical units. Colored tabs helped them distinguish medication records from laboratory and imaging reports. The process was slower than an electronic search, but essential work continued until the network returned. <u>When the computers restarted, the staff stopped using the drawers and resumed entering searches electronically.</u><b>[8]</b>"
