      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>The Question I Did Not Ask</b><br><br>For a community-history project, I volunteered to interview Ms. Chen, who had driven city buses for thirty-two years. I arrived with a recorder, two sharpened pencils, and a page of questions about vehicle models and route changes. <u>My finger rested beside the first question as if the paper could conduct the entire conversation for me.</u><b>[1]</b><br><br>Ms. Chen answered each question politely but briefly. The recorder's red light blinked while the space between us <u>manufactured</u><b>[2]</b> longer. Then she opened a folder and unfolded a route map covered with penciled stars<u>, none of the stars appeared in the printed legend.</u><b>[3]</b><br><br>I almost returned to my list. Instead, I asked what the stars meant. Ms. Chen touched each one and explained <u>their</u><b>[4]</b> purpose: the marks identified stops used by late-shift workers making the final connection home. <u>For example,</u><b>[5]</b> drivers on two routes sometimes radioed one another when a factory bus was delayed by a few minutes.<br><br>There was no official rule requiring the second driver to wait. Ms. Chen described the practice as <u>the most heroic transportation operation in the entire history of civilization.</u><b>[6]</b> A driver still had to consider the published schedule and other passengers, but a short delay could prevent someone from being stranded after midnight. She remembered one winter evening when she <u>had drove</u><b>[7]</b> slowly toward the transfer stop until headlights appeared behind her.<br><br>A cluster of penciled notes beside one star <u>were</u><b>[8]</b> faded beyond recognition. [A] Ms. Chen supplied the missing date from memory. [B] <u>I asked her to explain which parts she remembered clearly and which parts she was reconstructing.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] We recorded both the story and that distinction. [D] After the interview, I photographed the map and linked the image to the audio file.<br><br>My prepared questions gave the archive useful dates, route numbers, and equipment details. The penciled stars revealed something those categories had not anticipated: a small practice shaped by judgment, cooperation, and the needs of particular riders. I left the page of questions in the project folder, but I added one at the bottom for my next interview: What in the record still needs to be noticed?<b>[10]</b>"
