      passage: "<b>Reading the Wall</b><br><br><u>Standing on a scaffold at sunrise, the faded letters were examined by sign restorer Mara Vale</u><b>[1]</b>. Light striking the brick from the side revealed curves that disappeared when the sun rose higher. Before adding paint, Vale needed to determine which marks belonged to the earliest sign and which came from later advertisements.<br><br>Vale photographed the wall, traced surviving outlines onto clear sheets, and <u>compares</u><b>[2]</b> them with two storefront photographs from the city archive. A series of dark paint fragments near the upper border reveal that a narrow line once framed the lettering. Small pigment tests helped her distinguish the original blue layer <u>with</u><b>[3]</b> a later coat of green. One photograph also showed that a delivery wagon had been parked beside the building. Together, the evidence established the sign's wording and approximate dimensions.<br><br>A complete repaint offered an obvious attraction: it would <u>make the wall look brand-new and super sharp</u><b>[4]</b>. <u>Therefore</u><b>[5]</b>, such a repaint could cover cracks, faded brushstrokes, and other evidence of the sign's age. Leaving every fragment untouched posed a different risk because loose paint would continue to fall from the brick. <b>[6]</b><br><br>Vale selected a restrained treatment guided by the surviving evidence. Her plan <u>followed three rules, remove loose dirt, secure fragile paint, and retouch only documented gaps</u><b>[7]</b>. Where photographs clearly showed the shape of a letter, she added a thin layer of matching pigment. Where the design remained uncertain, she left the brick exposed.<br><br>When the scaffold came down, the advertisement was legible but still weathered. Viewers could identify the old business name without mistaking the wall for a newly manufactured sign. <u>The restoration made the surviving design easier to read while preserving the gaps that recorded its age</u><b>[8]</b>."
