      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Making Rubber Reliable</b><br><br>Natural rubber begins as latex, a milky liquid produced by certain plants. Latex contains tiny rubber particles suspended in water<u>, when the liquid is collected and treated, those particles can join into a flexible solid.</u><b>[1]</b> As water is removed, the particles <u>congregate</u><b>[2]</b> into a continuous mass. The resulting material stretches and resists water, qualities that made it useful long before industrial manufacturing.<br><br>Communities in Mesoamerica learned to process latex into durable objects, including balls and water-resistant goods. <u>These uses show that rubber technology did not begin with nineteenth-century European factories.</u><b>[3]</b> When European manufacturers later obtained natural rubber, they tried to adapt <u>their</u><b>[4]</b> unusual properties to shoes, coats, and containers.<br><br>The material presented a stubborn problem. Untreated rubber softened and became sticky in hot weather, while cold temperatures made it stiff. <u>In contrast,</u><b>[5]</b> an object that worked well in one season might become unusable in another. During the early nineteenth century, manufacturers <u>experiment</u><b>[6]</b> with powders, solvents, and surface coatings <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[7]</b> make rubber more stable. The long molecules can also move enough for untreated rubber to stretch and partly regain its shape.<br><br>Heating rubber with sulfur produced a more dependable result<u>, the treated material remained elastic across a wider range of temperatures.</u><b>[8]</b> Heat helps sulfur form links between long rubber molecules. Those links limit how freely the molecules can slide past one another, reducing both stickiness and brittleness.<br><br>[A] The process became known as vulcanization. [B] <u>Manufacturers could adjust the treatment for products requiring different degrees of flexibility and strength.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] Softer rubber could cushion moving parts, while harder formulations could support rigid objects. [D] Later improvements made production more consistent and expanded the range of useful rubber goods.<br><br>Vulcanization did not create the useful qualities of natural rubber; it made those qualities dependable under more conditions. By overcoming the material's sensitivity to temperature, the process helped transform rubber from a difficult raw substance into a practical component of belts, seals, hoses, tires, and countless other products.<b>[10]</b>"
