    76: {
      skill_tested: "Joining independent clauses with a coordinating conjunction",
      question: "(1) Which choice makes the sentence most grammatically acceptable?",
      option1: "the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form",
      option2: "and the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form",
      option3: "because the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form",
      option4: "the arrangement resembling an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form",
      correctAnswer: "and the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> The words before the underlined portion form an independent clause: “Each envelope holds seeds that neighborhood gardeners may borrow.”<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> Because a comma already appears before the underlined portion, the coordinating conjunction “and” is needed to connect that clause correctly to “the arrangement resembles an ordinary library.” The comma before “but” then correctly introduces the contrasting independent clause.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> “and the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form” correctly joins the clauses and preserves the intended meaning.",
      practiceYear: 2,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Sentence Structure",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Seeds in Circulation</b><br><br>At the Northgate Community Center, a narrow cabinet stands between the cookbooks and the gardening manuals. Its drawers contain envelopes rather than books. Each envelope holds seeds that neighborhood gardeners may borrow, <u>the arrangement resembles an ordinary library, but nothing is returned in its original form</u> <b>[1]</b>. Participants plant the seeds, allow several healthy plants to mature, and save part of the next generation. After harvest, they are asked <u>to return back a portion of the seeds that they harvested from those plants</u> <b>[2]</b>. These returns are not late fees or payments; they replenish varieties that other gardeners may borrow the following season.<br><br>In April, gardener Lena Ortiz selected a packet of pale-speckled beans described as productive during dry summers. She planted them along a brick wall, where the soil warmed early but received little afternoon rain. In a notebook, Ortiz recorded the planting date, the number of days before the first flowers appeared, and which plants remained vigorous during a two-week dry spell. At the end of the season, she chose pods from several healthy plants instead of relying on a single specimen. She placed the dried beans in an envelope <u>marked with the harvest date and planting conditions, information that could guide the next borrower</u> <b>[3]</b>. The notes did not guarantee identical results in another yard, but they gave future gardeners a useful starting point.<br><br>Records like Ortiz's also help coordinators observe how a variety performs across several seasons. Seeds reproduced repeatedly in one area may gradually become better suited to its clay-heavy soil, uneven summer rainfall, or common insect pests. No single harvest transforms a variety. Rather, gardeners preserve seeds from plants that performed well under local conditions, and those choices can accumulate over time. Maintaining several bean, squash, and pepper varieties further protects the collection: a pest or unusually wet spring may damage one variety while leaving another productive. Returned seeds therefore do more than <u>decorate</u> <b>[4]</b> the cabinet; they restore its supply and preserve multiple options for future planting.<br><br><u>As a result</u> <b>[5]</b>, locally reproduced seeds are not automatically reliable. A gardener may collect beans before they fully mature, place the wrong name on an envelope, or grow two easily crossed varieties too close together. Such mistakes can remain unnoticed until another participant plants the seeds months later. <b>[6]</b> During a brief orientation, coordinators demonstrate how to dry seeds thoroughly, identify plants suitable for saving, and follow isolation guidelines when cross-pollination is likely. Each return is reviewed by a volunteer coordinator, <u>and the staff member asks the gardener and them to confirm the variety name</u> <b>[7]</b>. <u>The cabinet is kept at precisely 16 degrees Celsius because that temperature is used by several professional seed banks</u> <b>[8]</b>. These procedures cannot eliminate every uncertain result, but they make the collection more dependable.<br><br>A seed library succeeds through repeated exchanges rather than through the size of its first donation. A packet may pass through several gardens, gathering observations about shade, rainfall, pests, and harvest time along the way. Yet access alone does not maintain that record. Participants must return viable seeds, label them accurately, and describe what occurred in their plots. <u>Under this system, borrowing a packet also means accepting responsibility for what may enter the drawer next</u> <b>[9]</b>."
    },
