      practiceYear: 2,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Punctuation",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>The Unnoticed Shelter</b><br><br>Along dry trails, hikers often regard the low thornbrush as little more than<u>, an obstacle to step around</u><b>[1]</b>. Its gray branches snag loose clothing, and for much of the year, the plant appears nearly lifeless. <u>Therefore</u><b>[2]</b>, its seed pods can remain sealed through months of heat and open after steady rain softens their outer shells. The seeds inside may <u>remain alive and continue to stay viable</u><b>[3]</b> through several dry seasons.<br><br>The plant also alters the ground immediately around it. The thornbrush survives partly because <u>its roots' ability to spread through loose soil protects the plants base</u><b>[4]</b>. Together, these materials <u>decorate</u><b>[5]</b> the soil against movement during sudden storms. The shrub's narrow shadow also lowers surface temperatures enough to protect some newly sprouted plants after fire has removed taller vegetation.<br><br>After a wildfire stripped an exposed ridge of grass and small trees, a restoration crew studied patches where thornbrush had survived. <u>After testing three planting methods across shaded and exposed plots, the seedlings in protected depressions were identified by the crew as the most likely to survive.</u><b>[6]</b>The crew then collected dry branches, marked shallow depressions that received afternoon shade, and placed seeds beneath thin layers of soil. Nearly half of the protected planting pockets still contained seedlings six months later.<br><br>Before seasonal rains arrived, wind began carrying ash and loose soil down the bare hillside. <u>The crew had only a few established shrubs remaining, they arranged cut branches across the slope as low windbreaks</u><b>[7]</b>. The branches slowed moving soil, and the depressions held enough moisture for more seeds to sprout. By the next growing season, grasses and flowering annuals had appeared between the young shrubs. <u>The thornbrush produced new plants, and the crew counted them during its final survey.</u><b>[8]</b>"
