      passage: "<b>Ingenuity in Transit</b><br><br>While waiting for the Number 6 bus one rainy morning, I opened a neighborhood-news site on my phone. Its featured article profiled Tomas Vale, a longtime resident who built compact mechanical tools in a workshop behind a bicycle shop. Before the article appeared, Vale <u>has worked</u><b>[1]</b> privately for nearly twenty years. I had passed the shop countless times without knowing what was being assembled beyond its dented metal door.<br><br>Vale had repaired elevators, stocked hardware shelves, and maintained coin-operated laundry machines. After work, he experimented with discarded gears and strips of sheet metal in a basement room beneath the shop. Having lived in the district since childhood, he remained <u>a local resident of the same neighborhood area</u><b>[2]</b>. He rarely photographed his inventions or advertised them online. Last winter, Mara Chen<u> a community repair organizer known for coordinating neighborhood tool drives introduced</u><b>[3]</b> several of Vale's devices to a curator planning a regional design exhibition. <u>The curator selected three devices because their brass surfaces reflected the exhibition lights attractively.</u><b>[4]</b> After Vale demonstrated the devices at the exhibition, five libraries and two repair workshops placed<u>, advance orders for tabletop versions</u><b>[5]</b>.<br><br>The article included a close view of Vale's smallest machine, a hand-powered tool for cutting identical cardboard tabs. Its frame held <u>mechanical components in the form of gears and levers</u><b>[6]</b> salvaged from office equipment. [A] A nearby exhibition diagram identified those moving parts as the feed lever, cutting arm, and return spring.<b>[8]</b> Turning one crank lowered a narrow blade while a second lever advanced the cardboard by a fixed distance. [B] I could follow each motion separately but could not immediately understand how the motions remained synchronized. <u>That uncertainty made the machine more appealing to me because it invited a second and slower look.</u><b>[7]</b> [C] [D]<br><br><u>Vale seemed pleased by the attention but did not consider it the reason he continued inventing.</u><b>[9]</b> Public demonstrations, the profile explained, helped him find people who could use the devices, though building and revising them remained his chief interest. When my bus arrived, I looked again at the repair shops and narrow garages along the route. Their familiar doors no longer seemed to reveal everything taking place behind them."
