      passage: "<b>Crossing Sideways</b><br><br><u>[1] As soon as my kayak left the sheltered cove, the current pushed its bow downstream. [2] On the final morning of a paddling course, our group would cross a narrow channel to a gravel bar. [3] I had practiced stopping and turning on still water, so I expected to point directly at the bar and paddle across.</u><b>[1]</b><br><br>Our instructor, Maya, demonstrated first. She <u>attacked</u><b>[2]</b> the moving water with her bow angled upstream. Her paddle kept a steady rhythm<u>, the kayak traveled diagonally and reached the bar without being swept below it.</u><b>[3]</b> From shore, the maneuver looked almost ordinary.<br><br>At Maya's signal, I aimed at the gravel bar itself. Each stroke moved me forward, but the current carried me sideways faster than I realized. <u>Maya pointed to a pale boulder on the opposite bank and told me to watch whether it shifted behind my bow.</u><b>[4]</b> Each paddler could use <u>their</u><b>[5]</b> chosen landmark to see drift that was difficult to feel from the boat.<br><br>I turned back before reaching the main channel. By then, I <u>had went</u><b>[6]</b> far enough downstream to understand why greater effort in the same direction would not solve the problem. Maya asked me to choose an upstream angle <u>with the aim of possibly managing to</u><b>[7]</b> balance the current. A line of bubbles beside the kayak <u>were</u><b>[8]</b> moving steadily toward the bend.<br><br>[A] I studied the boulder again. [B] <u>This time, I pointed the bow several degrees upstream from the gravel bar.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] I paddled at the same pace I had used in the cove, checking the boulder after every few strokes. [D] The bow still moved sideways, but now the sideways movement carried me toward the landing instead of past it. When the hull touched gravel, I laughed at how little the crossing had resembled a straight line.<br><br>On still water, the place I aimed and the place I traveled had seemed identical. The current separated those ideas. I crossed successfully only after treating drift as information and choosing an angle that accounted for it. Sometimes the most direct progress begins by pointing somewhere other than the visible destination.<b>[10]</b>"
