      practiceYear: 3,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Conciseness and Redundancy",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Another Turn</b><br><br>On Saturday morning, I followed my aunt into a recreation-center room crowded with lamps, clocks, and kitchen appliances. A hand-painted sign called the gathering a repair cafe. I had brought a desk fan that had stopped spinning, though I felt <u>completely certain without any doubt</u><b>[1]</b> that the volunteers would pronounce it useless. At home, replacing a small appliance had always seemed easier than opening it.<br><br>At the first table, a volunteer named Mr. Alvarez listened to a radio that produced only a low hum. He did not immediately reach for a screwdriver. Instead, he asked its owner when the problem began and whether the sound changed as the dial turned. His questions <u>manufactured</u><b>[2]</b> a trail from symptom to possible cause. Nearby, a child sorted loose buttons by size while her father restitched a coat lining. The activity around the tables <u>were</u><b>[3]</b> steady and purposeful; each group seemed organized around deliberate attention.<br><br><u>Nevertheless,</u><b>[4]</b> when my turn came, I placed the fan on a rubber mat. Mr. Alvarez showed me how to test whether electricity reached the motor. The current was present, so we unplugged the fan and removed its cover. <u>The cover was a pale shade of gray.</u><b>[5]</b> A thread had wound tightly around the shaft, preventing the blades from turning. I expected him to pull it free himself<u>, he handed</u><b>[6]</b> me a pair of tweezers instead.<br><br>[A] The thread resisted at first. [B] I loosened one loop, then another, while Mr. Alvarez held a flashlight above the motor. [C] When the final strand came away, I replaced the cover and pressed the switch. [D] <u>Only then did the blades begin moving again.</u><b>[7]</b> The fan's breeze scattered two paper receipts across the table, and I <u>laugh</u><b>[8]</b> as my aunt caught them.<br><br>Before leaving, I wrote the repair on a card<u>; fan would not turn, thread removed from motor shaft, tested successfully</u><b>[9]</b>. The card would help another volunteer recognize a similar problem. I had entered the room thinking that repair meant delaying an inevitable purchase. I left carrying the same fan<u>—and a willingness to look closely before giving up on the next broken thing.</u><b>[10]</b>"
