      passage: "<b>Night Maps for City Trees</b><br><br>Street trees cool sidewalks, but the hottest blocks can also place the greatest strain on them. During the day, sunlight warms leaves, pavement, and building walls<u>, after sunset, however, those surfaces release heat at different rates.</u><b>[1]</b> A daytime photograph may therefore miss stress that becomes visible only at night.<br><br>To find that stress, urban foresters can pair thermal images with measurements of soil moisture. <u>One crew, for example, mapped the same neighborhood shortly after sunset and again before dawn.</u><b>[2]</b> Trees whose canopies remained unusually warm were often growing beside broad areas of pavement. Sensors beneath several of those trees also recorded <u>meager</u><b>[3]</b> moisture.<br><br>The two kinds of evidence do not prove that every warm tree needs water. <u>Similarly,</u><b>[4]</b> they help crews decide where to inspect first. Workers can then check roots, soil compaction, and signs of disease before choosing a response.<br><br>A thermal map is thus a guide rather than a diagnosis. By directing limited time toward the most vulnerable locations, <u>it</u><b>[5]</b> can help a city protect young trees before their decline is obvious from the street."
