      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Punctuation",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>A Better Kind of Streetlight</b><br><br>When electric streetlights became widespread, cities often treated brightness as a simple measure of safety. Fixtures were mounted high above roads, and many sent light toward bedroom windows and the sky as readily as toward the pavement. That approach once seemed <u>sensible, however, it now</u><b>[1]</b> wastes energy and creates glare that can hide pedestrians beyond the brightest patch. An unshielded lamp sometimes <u>throws a wild amount of light</u><b>[2]</b> far beyond the area it is meant to illuminate. Municipalities replacing old fixtures should choose shielded, adjustable lights instead of merely installing brighter ones.<br><br>A shield directs a lamp's beam downward, where people actually need it. <u>Some municipalities paint their lamp posts black, while others prefer dark green.</u><b>[3]</b> The metal hood around each bulb, along with its angled panels, <u>keep</u><b>[4]</b> light from spilling above the fixture. Motion sensors and programmable controls can then lower the output <u>during the nighttime hours when few people are present at night</u><b>[5]</b> and restore full illumination when traffic increases. These changes reduce wasted electricity without requiring a street to remain unlit.<br><br><u>Critics reasonably note that replacing every fixture at once would strain a municipal budget.</u><b>[6]</b> A staged replacement answers that concern<u>, it lets</u><b>[7]</b> a city install adaptive fixtures when old lamps fail or when a road is already scheduled for repairs. Because LEDs generally need less frequent replacement than older bulbs, maintenance crews can also spend fewer hours closing lanes to change lamps. The savings accumulate gradually rather than depending on one dramatic project.<br><br>[A] Before changing an entire district, officials should ask residents and businesses where glare or dim areas cause problems. [B] They can then operate a small group of new fixtures for several weeks. [C] Residents can compare visibility before and after the change, while emergency responders can identify corners that still need adjustment. [D] <u>Only after reviewing those observations should the council approve a wider installation.</u><b>[8]</b> The evaluation should focus on three goals<u>; clear sidewalks, darker bedroom windows, and lower electric bills</u><b>[9]</b>. A successful lighting policy is not the one that produces the most light; it is the one that puts an appropriate amount of light where people need it.<b>[10]</b>"
