      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Punctuation",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Climbing the Pass</b><br><br>At dawn, mule teams once waited beside warehouses in the isolated mining settlement of Alder Gap. Ore had to descend nearly forty miles by rough wagon road, while machinery and food traveled uphill along the same route. Local merchants therefore financed <u>a narrow-gauge railway, a freight line intended for the mountain settlements, across the pass</u><b>[1]</b>. Once completed, the railway reduced a journey of several days to less than one.<br><br><u>After surveying the cliffs for a workable route, the steep terrain forced the engineers to abandon a direct ascent</u><b>[2]</b>. Instead, crews <u>graded embankments, building short tunnels, and installed switch tracks</u><b>[3]</b>. At each switchback, the locomotive stopped and reversed direction before continuing upward. This arrangement kept the slope gradual enough to prevent trains <u>of gaining</u><b>[4]</b> excessive speed on the descent. Along exposed ledges, workers constructed fitted-stone retaining walls that supported the track bed.<br><br>The line carried ore, timber, mail, and passengers through every season. <u>For example</u><b>[5]</b>, year-round operation demanded constant attention. A network of tunnels, walls, and timber snow sheds <u>require</u><b>[6]</b> inspection after storms. Crews removed fallen rock, replaced warped beams, and shoveled drifts away from tunnel entrances. <u>One foreman was known for playing cards with the station clerk after his shift</u><b>[7]</b>. Even with these precautions, winter closures sometimes delayed freight for days.<br><br>Decades later, a longer railway with gentler grades allowed heavier trains to cross the range without reversing direction. The original line was abandoned, and several sections became a walking trail. Stone walls and two tunnel portals remain beside the path. <u>Rather than resembling a complete railway, these fragments allow visitors to trace how engineers once negotiated the mountain</u><b>[8]</b>."
