    174: {
      skill_tested: "Joining related independent clauses",
      question: "(1) Which choice makes the sentence most grammatically acceptable?",
      option1: ", a university research team",
      option2: "; a university research team",
      option3: ": although a university research team",
      option4: "a university research team",
      correctAnswer: "; a university research team",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> The statements about astronomers and the research team can each stand alone.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> A semicolon correctly joins the closely related independent clauses.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> ; a university research team",
      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Sentence Structure",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Listening to the Data</b><br><br>Astronomers often examine measurements as points, curves, or colors on a screen<u>, a university research team</u><b>[1]</b> also converts some measurements into sound. This process, called sonification, assigns audible features to numerical values. <u>During an early demonstration, a listener noticed a brief cluster of high notes that was easy to overlook as a narrow spike on the projected graph.</u><b>[2]</b> The observation showed the team that listening could reveal patterns deserving closer visual inspection.<br><br>To build a sonification, the researchers first decide which relationships matter. Time may move from left to right on a graph and forward through a recording. A rising measurement might produce a higher pitch, while a stronger signal controls volume. The team maps these features<u>—with rules, that remain</u><b>[3]</b> consistent throughout a recording. Consistency allows a listener to recognize <u>good</u><b>[4]</b> changes without memorizing every value. <u>After selecting a dataset, the sounds are tested by the researchers with listeners before revising the mapping.</u><b>[5]</b><br><br>Several measurements can change at once. <u>In addition,</u><b>[6]</b> the team avoids assigning a separate instrument to every column of data. Too many simultaneous sounds can conceal the pattern they are meant to express. Instead, the researchers choose three primary features<u>; pitch for frequency, volume for intensity, and duration for the length of an event</u><b>[7]</b>. This limited musical vocabulary helps listeners <u>translate</u><b>[8]</b> a complicated table into a sequence they can compare over time.<br><br>One recording represents repeated pulses from a distant object with evenly spaced tones; a slight delay appears as one tone arriving later than expected. Another turns changes in a star's brightness into a slow rise and fall of pitch. Neither recording replaces the original measurements. Researchers return to the numbers to test any pattern they hear. The team's guiding principle is concise: <u>“Sound is not an answer added to the data; it is another route into the question.”</u><b>[9]</b> By making numerical relationships available to both sight and hearing, sonification expands how researchers can notice, compare, and investigate them.<b>[10]</b>"
    },
