      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Hard",
      module: "Sentence Structure",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Current of Voices</b><br><br>Inside a darkened gallery, a pale line of projected water bends across the floor. Four speakers mounted near the walls play the voices of a boat builder, scientist, farmer, and student. <u>The recordings overlap, visitors hear what sounds like a conversation moving around the room</u><b>[1]</b>. No speaker appears on a screen, so the exchange initially seems detached from any particular year.<br><br><u>For example</u><b>[2]</b>, the installation provides clues beneath visitors' feet. <b>[3]</b> [A] Dates glow beside bends in the projected river, and arrows link each date to a short caption about navigation, flooding, industry, or habitat. [B] By following the lights in sequence, visitors can reconstruct a timeline even when they enter the gallery midway through the program. [C] The room then darkens briefly before the next voice begins. [D]<br><br>The voices seem to respond directly to one another. <u>Likewise</u><b>[4]</b>, each recording was made separately, sometimes months apart. The <u>designers</u><b>[5]</b> arrangement <u>combines the recordings together into one sequence</u><b>[6]</b> and places related statements side by side: a boat builder recalls crowded freight landings, and a scientist describes monitoring oxygen levels near the same shoreline many decades later.<br><br>The contrast is not merely chronological. One caption explains that warehouses once depended on deep channels for shipping; another notes that abandoned pilings later became sheltered areas for young fish. <u>A proposed caption explains that workers often waited beside the freight landings for incoming boats.</u><b>[7]</b><br><br>Contemporary voices complicate the sequence further. A farmer describes levees that protect fields while limiting where floodwater can spread. The student explains a neighborhood sampling project in which volunteers test water after heavy rain. Referring to earlier pollution, the student says that the river had become <u>really gross and nasty</u><b>[8]</b> before cleanup efforts began.<br><br>As each account plays, the gallery brightens near the corresponding section of the map and dims elsewhere. <u>The shifting light prevents any one speaker or period from appearing to define the river permanently</u><b>[9]</b>."
