    193: {
      skill_tested: "Repairing a dangling introductory modifier",
      question: "(1) Which choice makes the sentence most grammatically acceptable?",
      option1: "Looking across the intersection, the wall's lowest section seemed likely to be blocked by parked delivery trucks",
      option2: "Looking across the intersection, Cho realized that parked delivery trucks would likely block the wall's lowest section",
      option3: "Looking across the intersection, parked delivery trucks seemed likely to block the wall's lowest section",
      option4: "Looking across the intersection, the mural's lowest section was likely to be blocked by parked delivery trucks",
      correctAnswer: "Looking across the intersection, Cho realized that parked delivery trucks would likely block the wall's lowest section",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> The introductory phrase “Looking across the intersection” must logically describe the subject that immediately follows it.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> Cho is the person looking across the intersection. The other choices illogically suggest that the wall, trucks, or mural performed the action of looking.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> “Looking across the intersection, Cho realized that parked delivery trucks would likely block the wall's lowest section” correctly connects the modifier to Cho.",
      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Hard",
      module: "Modifiers and Comparisons",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Reworking the Corner Wall</b><br><br>On a Saturday morning, artist Lena Cho pressed strips of blue tape against a blank wall beside the Mercer Avenue bus stop. The tape marked the edges of a mural planned by neighborhood residents. <u>Looking across the intersection, the wall's lowest section seemed likely to be blocked by parked delivery trucks</u><b>[1]</b>. Cho therefore shifted the main images upward before any paint was mixed.<br><br>The first design showed a detailed street map stretching across the wall, <u>several residents argued that the narrow lines would be difficult to see from passing buses</u><b>[2]</b>. Others wanted the map retained because it showed the locations of former shops and gathering places. To test both concerns, the artists painted two sample panels on plywood and placed <u>them</u><b>[3]</b> at the bus stop, where viewers compared the designs from the sidewalk and across the street.<br><br><u>For example</u><b>[4]</b>, the larger map proved legible at a distance, but it left little room for the handwritten memories residents had collected. The group eventually chose a simpler street grid bordered by short quotations. <b>[5]</b> Cho also replaced several delicate painted lines with ceramic tiles that could withstand rain and repeated cleaning.<br><br>Before installation, volunteers sorted the quotations by subject rather than by speaker. Phrases about work, food, transportation, and neighborhood change appeared beside the locations they described. One proposed quotation was <u>completely removed and taken out altogether</u><b>[6]</b> because no one could verify its source. Another was shortened to fit a tile without altering its meaning.<br><br>When the mural opened, the finished wall did not reproduce the original drawing exactly. That difference was not treated as a failure. <u>The project had gone through a bunch of changes because people kept having opinions</u><b>[7]</b>. <u>Instead, the completed mural recorded both the neighborhood's history and the decisions required to make that history visible in a public place</u><b>[8]</b>."
    },
