      correctAnswer: "but several residents argued that the narrow lines would be difficult to see from passing buses",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> The clause before the underlined portion and the clause beginning with “several residents” are both independent clauses, so they cannot be joined with only a comma.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> The residents' concern contrasts with the proposed detailed map: although the design included narrow lines, residents believed those lines would be difficult to see from buses. The coordinating conjunction “but” correctly joins the clauses and expresses that contrast. “Because” would illogically suggest that the residents' later argument caused the first design to exist, while option 4 creates an incomplete construction.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> “but several residents argued that the narrow lines would be difficult to see from passing buses” creates a grammatical compound sentence and clearly expresses the contrast.",
