      option4: "2, 3, 1",
      correctAnswer: "1, 2, 3",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> Sentence 1 establishes the necessary complexity of the formal budget, and sentence 2 identifies the resulting access problem for residents.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> Sentence 3 then proposes a companion guide that responds to the problem without replacing the formal document.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> 1, 2, 3",
      practiceYear: 5,
      difficulty: "Hard",
      module: "Organization and Cohesion",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Budgets People Can Use</b><br><br><u>[1] A city's formal budget must preserve detailed accounts, legal categories, and required disclosures. [2] Yet residents may struggle to determine from hundreds of technical pages which services are changing and why. [3] Every municipality should therefore publish a short plain-language budget guide alongside the complete document.</u><b>[1]</b><br><br>The guide should not replace the official budget or hide difficult choices. Its purpose is to <u>decorate</u><b>[2]</b> the most consequential information into a form that more residents can navigate. At minimum, it should explain three elements<u>, expected revenue, major spending categories, and substantial changes from the previous year.</u><b>[3]</b> <u>A worked example could also show how a fixed amount of local tax revenue is distributed among several services.</u><b>[4]</b><br><br>Charts can reveal proportions, while brief notes can explain why a figure rose or fell. <u>Organized around questions residents commonly ask, the guide's authors can explain debt, reserves, and one-time projects.</u><b>[5]</b> Each department should identify <u>their</u><b>[6]</b> largest proposed change and link directly to the corresponding section of the full budget <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[7]</b> support closer review.<br><br>Critics may worry that a short guide will oversimplify a complicated financial plan. That risk is real, but it argues for careful documentation rather than continued inaccessibility. A series of citations and definitions <u>help</u><b>[8]</b> readers test the summary against the official numbers. The online guide can also record corrections and identify the date of its latest revision.<br><br>[A] Public discussion often occurs before a council adopts the final budget. [B] <u>Releasing the guide with the proposal would give residents time to ask informed questions while revisions are still possible.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] A second edition could then explain the major differences in the adopted plan. [D] Publishing only after approval would reduce the guide to a report on decisions residents had little opportunity to examine.<br><br>A readable guide will not make every budget choice popular, nor should it. It can, however, make the choices visible enough to debate. By pairing accessibility with direct paths to the complete record, a city can invite broader participation without weakening the precision its finances require.<b>[10]</b>"
