      passage: "<b>The Line of the Wheel</b><br><br>My father taught me to care for his old blue touring bicycle when I was young. He showed me how to patch tubes, oil chains, and adjust brakes. He was my first mechanic <u>and the person whose patience I tried to imitate.</u><b>[1]</b> When he later gave me the bicycle, I kept a repair kit beneath the seat.<br><br>One Saturday, the chain began jumping whenever I pedaled uphill. My usual adjustments did nothing, so I wheeled the bicycle into a neighborhood repair cooperative. Along the <u>co-ops tool wall</u><b>[2]</b> hung wrenches and unfamiliar gauges. I wanted to replace the chain, but a volunteer named Lena <u>attacked</u><b>[3]</b> the problem more carefully.<br><br>She clamped the bicycle into a stand and asked me to turn the pedals. The rear wheel moved smoothly, but the chain wandered across the gears. Lena attached an alignment gauge near the derailleur. Lena and <u>me</u><b>[4]</b> compared four readings around the rim. <u>At that point in time,</u><b>[5]</b> I understood why adjusting the cable had never solved the problem: the metal hanger supporting the derailleur was slightly bent.<br><br>Lena demonstrated how to apply pressure gradually and measure after every small movement. <u>Meanwhile,</u><b>[6]</b> I copied each reading onto a scrap of cardboard. The numbers drew closer together until the hanger stood <u>more straighter than before.</u><b>[7]</b> When I turned the pedals again, the chain traveled quietly from one gear to the next. The repair had required less force than restraint.<br><br>[A] I kept the cardboard in my repair kit. [B] It reminded me that a symptom and its cause may be several adjustments apart. [C] A month later, I returned to the cooperative for its volunteer orientation. [D] <u>The lesson had changed how I approached unfamiliar repairs.</u><b>[8]</b> Since that afternoon, I <u>volunteered</u><b>[9]</b> at several open-shop sessions, often helping visitors slow down long enough to observe before replacing a part.<br><br>The blue bicycle still reminds me of the afternoons when my father taught me the basics. Now it also carries the memory of a crowded tool wall and a set of measurements made one careful turn at a time. Caring for it no longer means proving that I can solve every problem alone; it means knowing how patience and shared knowledge can keep a familiar wheel moving.<b>[10]</b>"
