      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Keeping a Palm House Alive</b><br><br>In the mid-nineteenth century, botanical gardens wanted to display tropical palms that could not survive a cold winter outdoors. A conventional masonry building admitted too little light and left limited room for tall fronds. Designers therefore planned a vast enclosure made chiefly of iron and glass. The goal required builders to admit abundant sunlight while spanning enough height and width for specimens expected to grow for decades.<br><br>Workers <u>coaxed</u><b>[1]</b> wrought-iron ribs into sweeping curves<u>, rows of small glass panes covered the framework and admitted sunlight from several directions.</u><b>[2]</b> <u>Techniques associated with shipbuilding helped craftsmen form the iron into a broad interior without heavy stone columns.</u><b>[3]</b> Heated air traveled through ducts beneath the floor, allowing the palm collection to maintain <u>their</u><b>[4]</b> warmer climate.<br><br>The light structure was elegant, but it required persistent maintenance. Moist air encouraged iron to corrode, and thousands of joints and panes created paths for heat to escape. <u>Nevertheless,</u><b>[5]</b> periodic painting and small repairs could not indefinitely correct damage hidden inside the framework.<br><br>During a major restoration, workers <u>have begun</u><b>[6]</b> by documenting each section before removing glass and weakened metal. They labeled reusable pieces <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[7]</b> return them to their original positions. A series of temporary enclosures <u>were</u><b>[8]</b> used to protect plants that could not be moved far. Corroded iron was repaired or replaced, and new glazing improved the building's ability to retain heat.<br><br>[A] The project also updated ventilation, controls, and service routes. [B] <u>These systems were fitted around the historic structure rather than allowed to dominate its appearance.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] Visitors could again read the rhythm of the curved ribs and repeated panes. [D] Gardeners, meanwhile, gained finer control over temperature and moisture.<br><br>The restored palm house remains both an artifact and a working environment. Its iron frame records an era of ambitious construction, while its renewed climate systems support a changing collection of living plants. Preserving the building succeeds only when those two responsibilities remain joined.<b>[10]</b>"
