      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Pieces of Morning</b><br><br>My alarm sounded before sunrise because I had promised my friend Lina that I would help install a mural beside the neighborhood train station. When we arrived, the wall held only a penciled grid. On the pavement, volunteers opened crates filled with <u>small, tiny pieces</u><b>[1]</b> of blue, silver, and amber tile. I wondered how scraps no wider than my thumbnail could become anything recognizable.<br><br>The artist coordinating the project, Maya Chen, unrolled a paper plan and <u>threw</u><b>[2]</b> its enlarged outline onto the wall with a portable projector. The pattern of numbered shapes <u>were</u><b>[3]</b> confusing until she explained that each number corresponded to a crate. From across the courtyard, the design looked like a collection of empty islands. Up close, however, every island gave a volunteer a manageable section to complete.<br><br><u>Eventually,</u><b>[4]</b> Lina and I were assigned a band of pale tiles near the mural's lower edge. <u>Maya's work gloves had orange stitching across the wrists.</u><b>[5]</b> Our section curved gradually, so we could not simply line the pieces in straight rows. Lina spread mortar over a small area<u>, I pressed</u><b>[6]</b> tiles along the penciled arc. At first, I concentrated so narrowly on matching the line that I failed to notice the image taking shape around us.<br><br>[A] Sunlight reached the upper wall shortly after seven. [B] The silver tiles began reflecting thin streaks across the darker blue sections. [C] I stepped backward and recognized the outline of a river winding between buildings. [D] <u>For the first time, I could see how our narrow curve belonged to the larger scene.</u><b>[7]</b> Maya handed Lina <u>her cloth</u><b>[8]</b> so that Lina could wipe mortar from the tile faces before it dried.<br><br>By midmorning, the blank grid had become a bright map of the neighborhood<u>; roofs, trees, tracks, and water</u><b>[9]</b>. Our names would not appear on the wall, and no viewer would know which pieces we had placed. That no longer bothered me. I had arrived believing that one person's handful of tiles could hardly matter. I left understanding that the image existed precisely because many people had finished one small section well.<b>[10]</b>"
