      passage: "<b>Open the Public-Domain Image</b><br><br>A painting may be old enough for its underlying work to be in the public domain, yet access to it can remain limited. A visitor might see the original only by traveling, while a catalog offers an image too small for close study. <u>Nevertheless,</u><b>[1]</b> museums that have authority to publish their digital reproductions should release high-resolution files of public-domain works for open use.<br><br>A <u>totally amazing and gigantic</u><b>[2]</b> image can serve purposes that a thumbnail cannot<u>, a student can inspect a brushstroke, and a conservator can compare the surface across time.</u><b>[3]</b> <u>Close views may also reveal inscriptions, repairs, or material details that are difficult to see from behind a barrier.</u><b>[4]</b> Designers, teachers, and researchers can then use the same documented file rather than relying on an unattributed copy found elsewhere.<br><br>Some institutions <u>had released</u><b>[5]</b> public-domain images with clear reuse terms and descriptive metadata. Each museum can adapt <u>their</u><b>[6]</b> process to the rights, privacy, cultural sensitivity, and conservation concerns associated with a collection. File pages should identify the work, creator, date, institution, and known restrictions <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[7]</b> keep reuse connected to reliable information. A collection of downloadable sizes and formats <u>make</u><b>[8]</b> the files practical for different users.<br><br>Opponents may worry that open files will reduce licensing income or encourage misleading alterations. Museums should examine those concerns rather than dismiss them. However, withholding a usable image does not prevent inaccurate copies from circulating. Clear source links, stable identifiers, and visible reuse guidance make it easier for responsible users to preserve context.<br><br>[A] Institutions do not need to release every eligible image at once. [B] <u>A phased program can begin with well-documented works that are already digitized.</u><b>[9]</b> [C] Staff can track costs, corrections, downloads, and new educational uses before expanding the program. [D] This approach creates evidence for later decisions instead of treating either complete restriction or immediate universal release as the only option.<br><br>Open access does not transfer ownership of the physical object or erase the museum's expertise. It extends the reach of works whose copyright restrictions have ended while keeping trustworthy records attached to them. When legal authority and responsible documentation are in place, public-domain collections should be available to the public at a quality that permits genuine study and creation.<b>[10]</b>"
