      passage: "<b>A Street After Sunset</b><br><br><u>[1] Temporary evening closures can prompt reasonable questions about traffic and noise. [2] After nearby offices close, some downtown side streets carry little traffic but remain lined with empty curb space. [3] Cities should allow carefully managed night markets on selected streets.</u><b>[1]</b> A limited pilot can answer those concerns while giving residents and small vendors a useful gathering place.<br><br>A night market does not require permanent construction. Removable barriers can close one or two blocks, and numbered spaces can <u>spawn</u><b>[2]</b> an orderly row of food stalls, craft tables, and produce stands. <u>Arranged along both curbs, visitors can walk through a clear central lane.</u><b>[3]</b> Vendors bring compact tents and remove them when the market ends, allowing the street to return to ordinary use by morning.<br><br>The temporary format lowers a barrier for businesses that cannot afford a permanent storefront. A baker can test a new product, while a clothing maker can speak directly with customers about <u>its</u><b>[4]</b> preferences. <u>In addition,</u><b>[5]</b> concentrating several vendors in one place can draw enough visitors to benefit nearby restaurants and transit stops. The market becomes more than a collection of sales tables; it creates a predictable occasion for people to spend time downtown after work.<br><br>[A] Residents may still worry that deliveries, buses, or emergency vehicles will lose access. [B] Organizers can preserve a cross street, establish a vehicle lane, and limit amplified sound. [C] Delivery hours can also be scheduled before barriers are placed. [D] <u>A published operating plan would allow residents and drivers to know exactly when each restriction begins and ends.</u><b>[6]</b> A market committee that <u>meet</u><b>[7]</b> after every event can review complaints, cleanup time, and attendance before approving the next date.<br><br>Cities should not treat every quiet street as a suitable market site. A hospital entrance or heavily traveled bus corridor would be a <u>totally disastrous and unbelievably foolish choice</u><b>[8]</b>. Instead, officials should select locations using three practical criteria<u>; low evening traffic, nearby transit, and adequate space for emergency access</u><b>[9]</b>. With those safeguards, a temporary market can turn an underused block into a recurring public space without permanently surrendering the street's transportation role.<b>[10]</b>"
