      passage: "<b>Reading the Wind</b><br><br>[A] My cousin Tessa invited me to help a coastal weather club launch what she called an instrument kite. I pictured a bright toy tugging uselessly above the beach and wondered why the club did not simply <u>use instead a regular ordinary weather station</u><b>[1]</b>. On the sand, however, the kite looked less like a toy than a narrow white wing. A small sensor package hung beneath its line.<br><br>[B] Club leader Ren Ito lifted a handful of sand and let it stream between his fingers. Farther inland, the grains fell almost straight down; near the water, they moved <u>like smoke being pulled through an invisible doorway</u><b>[2]</b>. Ito explained that wind can change with height. The club members checked the kite's seams and returned <u>its</u><b>[3]</b> tools to a shared case before carrying the wing toward the open beach.<br><br><u>Tessa tied three short ribbons along the line so the team could see small changes in airflow.</u><b>[4]</b> <u>Watching the ribbons flatten toward the dunes, the launch direction was changed by Ito.</u><b>[5]</b> He asked us to turn until the wind struck the wing evenly. Tessa held the frame upright while I released more line. [C]<br><br>The first launch ended quickly. One side of the kite dipped<u>, it dragged</u><b>[6]</b> a wingtip through the sand. Ito shortened a bridle cord by two centimeters, changing the angle at which the line pulled the frame. Tessa <u>raises</u><b>[7]</b> the kite again. This time it climbed steadily, carrying the sensor above the warm air close to the beach and into the cooler flow overhead.<br><br>At first, every knot and adjustment had seemed fussy to me. <u>Gradually, however, the movements of the ribbons, wing, and line began to look like readable evidence rather than delay.</u><b>[8]</b> [D] <u>Before the launch, Ito had explained that the sensor would record temperature and wind speed at several heights.</u><b>[9]</b> When the kite returned, its measurements showed the temperature falling as it climbed. I had arrived expecting to watch a toy fly. I left understanding that the kite was a carefully balanced question sent upward—and that the wind answered through every change in its motion.<b>[10]</b>"
