    351: {
      skill_tested: "Ordering historical context, a preservation problem, and a response logically",
      question: "(1) Which sequence makes the opening paragraph most logical?",
      option1: "1, 3, 2",
      option2: "3, 1, 2",
      option3: "2, 1, 3",
      option4: "3, 2, 1",
      correctAnswer: "3, 1, 2",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> Sentence 3 introduces wax cylinders before sentence 1 explains why the surviving objects are difficult to play.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> Sentence 2 then presents the archive's response to that preservation problem.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> 3, 1, 2",
      practiceYear: 8,
      difficulty: "Hard",
      module: "Organization and Cohesion",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>Reading Sound Without a Needle</b><br><br><u>[1] Many surviving cylinders are now too cracked, warped, or soft to endure an ordinary phonograph needle. [2] One sound archive therefore began testing a way to recover their contents without touching their surfaces. [3] Before flat discs became standard, listeners often heard recorded voices and music from wax cylinders.</u><b>[1]</b> The archive hoped to preserve performances, speeches, and spoken recollections that existed nowhere else.<br><br>On each cylinder, a narrow groove bends in response to the original sound. Those variations <u>decorate</u><b>[2]</b> information about pitch, volume, and timing. Playing the cylinder converts the groove back into vibration, but the pressure of a needle can damage a fragile recording. The archive's alternative begins with light rather than physical contact.<br><br>A scanning device photographs the groove from many angles. A sequence of high-resolution images <u>reveal</u><b>[3]</b> ridges, gaps, and deformations along the surface. Software then joins those images into a three-dimensional map<u>, the program follows the groove and estimates how a needle would have moved through it.</u><b>[4]</b> From that simulated movement, the system produces a digital sound file.<br><br>The first reconstruction may contain clicks or stretches caused by cracks in the wax. Technicians mark those damaged areas instead of silently erasing them. <u>For example,</u><b>[5]</b> later listeners can distinguish a defect in the cylinder from a pause in the original performance. [A] The archive also records the cylinder's label, dimensions, storage history, and scanning settings. [B] These notes allow another technician to repeat the process. [C] They also keep the sound connected to the physical object from which it came. [D] <u>A shared record of each decision makes the reconstruction open to review.</u><b>[6]</b><br><br>Once the files are cataloged, the cylinders no longer need to be handled whenever someone wants to hear <u>its</u><b>[7]</b> contents. Researchers can compare several recordings <u>in order to be able to</u><b>[8]</b> study changes in a singer's phrasing or a speaker's pronunciation. Since the archive opened its online portal, teachers and musicians <u>used</u><b>[9]</b> recordings that were previously available only in a reading room.<br><br>Optical scanning does not make the wax cylinder unimportant. Instead, it protects the object while revealing what the object carries. The method preserves more than a stream of sound: it joins an audible performance to the material evidence and documented choices that made its recovery possible.<b>[10]</b>"
    },
