      passage: "<b>Beneath the Blue Paint</b><br><br>[A] I was waiting for my sister in the workshop of the Bellweather Theater when I noticed a long wooden sign resting across two padded tables. Its blue surface was split near one corner, and several letters had faded almost completely. I assumed it was waiting to be discarded <u>away</u><b>[1]</b>. The theater's conservator, Ms. Okafor, corrected me: the sign had hung above the building's entrance for most of a century.<br><br>[B] She directed a lamp across the surface and invited me closer. Under the blue paint, thin shapes appeared like <u>sleepy lines barely waking beneath ice</u><b>[2]</b>. Ms. Okafor explained that the sign had been repainted whenever the building changed owners. The theater companies chose new names and covered the old ones, but traces of <u>its</u><b>[3]</b> lettering remained in the shallow grain.<br><br>Using a cotton swab dampened with solvent, she tested a square no larger than a postage stamp. <u>The first exposed letter ended in a curling stroke unlike the theater's current block lettering.</u><b>[4]</b> <u>Leaning over the table, the gold edge was revealed by Ms. Okafor one millimeter at a time.</u><b>[5]</b> A second curved line emerged beneath it. Together, the marks formed part of the word Lyric, the name of a music hall that had occupied the building decades before the present theater.<br><br>[C] I asked why she did not simply uncover the oldest layer and remove everything above it. Ms. Okafor shook her head<u>, each coat</u><b>[6]</b> belonged to the sign's history. Choosing one era would erase evidence of the others. Instead, she planned to reveal narrow windows into several layers while stabilizing the cracked wood. She <u>describes</u><b>[7]</b> the finished sign as a timeline that could be read without flattening its past into one date.<br><br>At first, the overlapping names had looked like damage to me. <u>Gradually, however, the exposed fragments began to resemble a conversation among the building's former lives.</u><b>[8]</b> [D] <u>A photograph in the theater archive showed the Lyric sign glowing above a crowd in 1948.</u><b>[9]</b> When my sister arrived, I stepped around the table once more before leaving. I no longer saw a ruined blue board; I saw a surface whose worn places had learned to hold more than one story.<b>[10]</b>"
