    19: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated purpose of a work practice.",
      question: "According to Passage B, why does Rosa leave narrow gaps among boxes?",
      option1: "To keep each pantry's frozen products separate from the dry goods already assigned to it",
      option2: "To make heavily loaded carts easier for the arriving drivers to identify and inspect",
      option3: "To reserve space for labels that volunteers will attach.",
      option4: "To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes",
      correctAnswer: "To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes",
      explanation: "Step 1: Rosa describes the gaps as handles made of air.<br><br>Step 2: The final paragraph confirms that they allow boxes to be shifted without unloading a whole cart.<br><br>Answer: To provide places where volunteers can grip and shift boxes",
      practiceYear: 1,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe narrators learning how public services are coordinated.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>From the passenger deck, a ferry terminal appears to operate by bells. One sounds, a gate opens, and people move toward a boat. During my first week in the operations room, I learned that the bell came near the end of a much longer sentence. Before it rang, arrival times, vehicle counts, tides, crew hours, and connecting buses had already been arranged into agreement.<br><br>Leah, the dispatcher training me, kept a pencil tucked behind her ear despite having three computer screens. She used it to mark a paper grid listing each crossing. I assumed the grid was a backup. Instead, she called it her horizon. The screens showed where vessels were now; the grid showed which later departures would be disturbed by any decision made now.<br><br>Late one afternoon, fog slowed an incoming ferry while a bus carrying forty passengers approached the terminal. Holding the next boat would preserve the connection but delay commuters already waiting. Sending it on time would strand the bus passengers for an hour. Leah asked the dock supervisor how quickly vehicles could unload, checked when one crew member's shift ended, and moved a smaller vessel to a later route. She then held the boat for six minutes.<br><br>Passengers in the waiting room heard only the revised departure announcement. Some sighed. They could not see that six minutes had protected a bus connection without pushing a crew beyond its permitted hours or leaving the later route uncovered. Leah made two pencil marks and watched the fog erase the far end of the pier.<br><br>I had thought dispatching meant responding quickly to whatever happened. Leah showed me that speed was less important than seeing how far a response traveled. By the end of the month, I had begun drawing my own paper horizon. The bell still sounded like a beginning downstairs. Upstairs, I heard it as confirmation that many possible beginnings had already been considered.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The distribution center looked calm at seven in the morning. Pallets formed square aisles, labels faced outward, and empty carts stood nested by the door. At nine, vans from six neighborhood pantries would arrive for weekly food orders. I was helping Rosa check the loads and wondered why she moved so slowly when there was so much to finish.<br><br>Rosa read each order from bottom to top. Heavy boxes were loaded first, but that was not her only rule. She separated glass jars from metal cans, kept items needed at the first delivery closest to the van door, and left narrow gaps where volunteers could grip the boxes. She called the gaps handles made of air. To me, they looked like wasted space.<br><br>At eight fifteen, a freezer alarm sounded. The maintenance worker estimated that repairs would take several hours. Rosa did not begin moving every frozen item. She checked the temperature log, identified the foods most likely to soften first, and called two pantries that had unused freezer capacity. Meanwhile, she reassigned volunteers so the ordinary dry-goods orders continued. The room grew louder, but the aisles remained open.<br><br>When the vans arrived, drivers took the threatened frozen foods to temporary storage along with their regular orders. No pantry received everything at its usual time, yet none lost a full delivery. Only after the last van left did Rosa explain that moving every frozen box at once would have blocked the aisles, delayed all six orders, and placed foods with different temperature limits into one undifferentiated emergency.<br><br>The empty gaps in the morning loads no longer looked careless. They allowed boxes to be shifted without unloading an entire cart. Rosa's slow checking had not postponed action; it had preserved options. I began to understand calm not as the absence of urgency but as a way of preventing one urgent problem from occupying all the available space."
    },
