    28: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated feature of a planning framework.",
      question: "According to the third paragraph, the categories of concentration, exchange, and production describe:",
      option1: "three architectural zones that planners are expected to include in every modern library.",
      option2: "successive stages through which a traditional reading room develops into a production space.",
      option3: "activities that may occur in different places or at different times.",
      option4: "specialized services that are practical only in large urban branches with separate rooms.",
      correctAnswer: "activities that may occur in different places or at different times.",
      explanation: "Step 1: The paragraph defines each category by what patrons do.<br><br>Step 2: It then states that the categories are not fixed rooms and gives a table that supports different activities at different times.<br><br>Answer: activities that may occur in different places or at different times.",
      practiceYear: 1,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how public libraries have expanded their functions while retaining older ones.<br><br>A visitor entering a public library at ten in the morning may encounter a room quieter than a chapel. Returning at four, the same visitor might find students building cardboard bridges, job seekers rehearsing interviews, and neighbors recording oral histories. Such variety is sometimes described as evidence that libraries have exchanged books for activities. That description mistakes addition for replacement. In many libraries, older and newer uses occupy the same building, sometimes uneasily, and each helps determine where the others can occur.<br><br>Early public-library buildings often expressed a straightforward sequence. A patron entered a grand reading room, requested material, and received it from controlled stacks. Later, open shelving allowed readers to browse independently. As circulating collections grew, architects devoted more floor space to aisles and less to monumental reading tables. These changes did not result from architecture alone. Longer school attendance, wider newspaper readership, and municipal investment brought people into libraries for reasons that could not be served by a single formal room.<br><br>Contemporary planners often describe library space through three overlapping functions: concentration, exchange, and production. Concentration requires protection from interruption. Exchange includes tutoring, discussion, and assistance that depend on conversation. Production involves making something, whether a printed poster, a recorded interview, or a repaired household object. The categories describe activities rather than fixed rooms. A table used for silent reading in the morning may host language practice in the evening.<br><br>Some libraries separate the functions with walls. A renovated branch in a former courthouse placed small study rooms along the building's thick outer corridor, where masonry muffled sound. It reserved the central hall for group programs and installed a workshop in what had been a records room. This arrangement reduced conflict, but it also created a new problem: newcomers could see the lively hall immediately while failing to discover the quiet rooms. Staff eventually marked the floor with colored paths leading to each type of space.<br><br>Other branches rely on time rather than walls. One rural library has a single large room, so its calendar alternates between quiet periods and collaborative programs. The arrangement is inexpensive and flexible, but a patron who can visit only during a scheduled activity may lose access to concentration. To address that limitation, the library lends noise-reducing headphones and allows patrons to reserve two desks in an adjoining municipal office. The solution is imperfect, yet it demonstrates that a small building can distribute functions beyond its own walls.<br><br>Production spaces have attracted particular attention because their tools are visible and unusual. A row of sewing machines or a compact recording booth can make institutional change seem dramatic. Their deeper effect, however, may be organizational. Staff members who once answered primarily reference questions now help patrons plan projects, locate instructors, and document finished work. At the same time, demand for books has not vanished. A workshop participant may consult a repair manual, while an oral-history group may search local newspapers before recording an interview. New activity can therefore increase the use of older collections.<br><br>The framework also changes how planners measure use. Counting only borrowed books overlooks a student who studies for hours, while attendance alone can make a crowded event appear more valuable than a quiet research visit. Some systems therefore record reservations, assistance sessions, program attendance, and circulation separately. No single measure defines success; together, they reveal which functions a community actually requests.<br><br>The most successful plans do not assume that every library needs the same balance. A branch beside a university may protect more space for concentration; one near a transit station may emphasize brief assistance and exchange. Nor are the three functions always harmonious. Tools make noise, conversation travels, and silent rooms can appear unwelcoming. Treating these tensions as design questions is more useful than declaring that one version of the library has defeated another. The modern library is not becoming a single new thing. It is becoming more deliberate about arranging several things it has gradually learned to do."
    },
