    46: {
      skill_tested: "Identify a detail that explains the narrator's reaction.",
      question: "According to Passage A, why does the narrator initially feel more like an exhibit label than a useful visitor?",
      option1: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.",
      option2: "The apprentices disregard the narrator's repair instructions because they use newer methods in the renovated doorway.",
      option3: "The renovated theater has converted the former backstage work area into space for digital equipment and public displays.",
      option4: "Mara asks the narrator to describe the theater's old routines but not to remain.",
      correctAnswer: "Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.",
      explanation: "Step 1: The narrator expects identifying the wooden wheel to produce a sense of usefulness.<br><br>Step 2: Mara photographs the wheel and stores the explanation in an archive, making the narrator feel recorded as part of the past rather than involved in present work.<br><br>Answer: Mara records the narrator's explanation instead of using the old tool immediately.",
      practiceYear: 2,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators returning to institutions where they once worked.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>The side entrance of the Bellwether Theater still resisted being opened. I leaned my shoulder against it, just as I had during six seasons of arriving before dawn to paint scenery. The door finally yielded, but the familiar smell of sawdust and hot cables had been replaced by the mineral scent of fresh plaster. In the lobby, volunteers were fastening silver letters to a wall that now read BELLWETHER ARTS CENTER.<br><br>I had come because Mara, the new technical director, had asked whether I could identify several unlabeled tools before the theater reopened. She met me carrying a tablet instead of the ring of brass keys that Mr. Vale, my old supervisor, used to wear. As we crossed the stage, floor panels lit beneath our feet to mark safe paths through the darkness. I tried not to stare. Mr. Vale had taught us to navigate by counting paces and feeling for seams with our shoes.<br><br>Backstage, most of the tools were ordinary. I named a canvas stretcher, two hand drills, and a curved blade used to trim painted cloth. Then Mara uncovered a wooden wheel with a deep groove along its rim. I explained how two such wheels had once guided ropes above the stage. She listened, photographed the wheel, and entered my explanation into the building's digital archive. I expected to feel useful. Instead, I felt like a label being attached to an exhibit.<br><br>A group of teenage apprentices entered carrying sections of a half-built doorway. One student set a corner down too quickly, twisting the frame. Before Mara could speak, I showed them how to brace the joint with a temporary diagonal strip. The students crowded close as I measured, tapped the frame square, and removed the strip. When they lifted the doorway again, it held. A girl with blue chalk across her sleeve asked why the brace worked, and my hands answered before my voice did.<br><br>On my way out, I passed the wall of silver letters. Through the open auditorium doors, I could hear the apprentices arguing cheerfully about paint. The old brass keys were gone, and no one would cross the stage by counting Mr. Vale's careful paces. Yet the doorway stood straight because a method had traveled from his hands to mine and, for at least one afternoon, from mine to theirs. The building had not kept my past intact. It had made room for it to continue without looking the same.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When I volunteered at the municipal greenhouse in college, visitors rarely saw me. I watered seedlings before opening, mixed soil behind the propagation shed, and left as school groups began rattling the gate. Ten years later, I returned as an architect assigned to design a rainwater system for the greenhouse's expansion. I arrived with rolled plans, polished shoes, and the confidence of someone who had rehearsed every answer.<br><br>Mrs. Okafor, who had supervised the volunteers, was waiting beside the old cistern. She greeted me warmly and then ignored my plans. Instead, she asked me to help move trays before the afternoon heat. Soon soil marked my sleeves, and my polished shoes had acquired a border of mud. I found myself checking the undersides of leaves for pests, an old habit so automatic that Mrs. Okafor laughed when she noticed.<br><br>At the planning meeting, I presented calculations showing that water captured from the new roofs could supply nearly half the greenhouse's summer needs. The board members studied the diagrams politely. Then Mrs. Okafor placed one of my seedling trays on the table. She explained that I had once spent an entire season experimenting with ways to keep young plants alive during watering restrictions. The proposed system, she said, was not merely an architect's solution; it grew from knowledge acquired in this greenhouse.<br><br>Her story unsettled me at first. I had wanted the board to judge the design as professional work, not as an affectionate project by a former volunteer. But when questions began, the members asked about both the calculations and the plants. Because I understood where runoff collected and where seedlings dried fastest, I could connect each pipe on the plan to a specific need. The two kinds of knowledge strengthened rather than diminished each other.<br><br>After the meeting, Mrs. Okafor handed me the tray. Its seedlings were descendants of a variety I had helped cultivate. I had imagined returning in a new role would require leaving the earlier one outside the gate. Carrying the tray and my plans together, I understood that the greenhouse had not summoned me backward. It had shown me that the work I did now had roots I no longer needed to conceal."
    },
