    73: {
      skill_tested: "Identify the purpose of calibrated and repeated routes.",
      question: "According to Passage A, why do researchers calibrate instruments and repeat survey routes at comparable times?",
      option1: "To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings",
      option2: "To ensure that every neighborhood records the same temperature",
      option3: "To replace fixed weather stations with resident workshops",
      option4: "To determine how long each pedestrian remains outdoors",
      correctAnswer: "To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings",
      explanation: "Step 1: A single reading can be affected by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Step 2: Calibration and repeated routes help researchers determine whether a corridor is consistently hot.<br><br>Answer: To separate persistent heat patterns from unusual individual readings",
      practiceYear: 3,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider two approaches to mapping heat in cities.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>On a July afternoon, a sensor mounted on a city bus may record a temperature several degrees higher beside an asphalt lot than beneath a row of mature trees. Multiply that reading by thousands of locations, and planners obtain a thermal map: a picture of how heat is distributed across streets, roofs, parks, and blocks.<br><br>Such maps are useful because ordinary weather stations are sparse. A station at an airport can accurately report regional conditions while missing sharp variations within a neighborhood. Mobile surveys, satellite images, and fixed sensors fill that gap. Each method observes a different surface and interval. A satellite may cover the whole city on one cloudless afternoon, while a curbside sensor can sample the same block repeatedly. Researchers also record wind, recent rain, and sensor height because those conditions affect comparisons. The resulting map is therefore not a simple photograph; it is a set of measurements organized by an explicit protocol. When instruments are calibrated and routes are repeated at comparable times, researchers can distinguish a consistently hot corridor from an unusually warm reading caused by a stopped vehicle or reflected sunlight.<br><br>Yet a thermal map is not automatically a map of human exposure. A playground may be extremely hot at noon but empty then. A moderately warm bus stop may expose hundreds of riders who wait there each afternoon. Buildings also matter: an apartment can remain hot overnight even after its surrounding street cools. The measurement describes a physical condition at a particular place and time; it does not by itself reveal who encounters that condition, for how long, or with what alternatives.<br><br>For this reason, some planning teams combine temperature data with transit schedules, housing information, and pedestrian counts. The added layers do not make the original measurements less objective. They make the question more precise. Instead of asking only where the city is hottest, planners can ask where heat and unavoidable daily activity overlap.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>When residents of the East Ward were asked to mark the hottest parts of their neighborhood, they did not begin with the broad avenue that appeared darkest on the city's thermal map. They circled the laundromat where customers waited outside, the unsheltered crossing used by children after school, and a narrow path older residents took to a pharmacy.<br><br>Their map recorded movement as much as temperature. Workshop participants drew morning and evening routes in different colors. They noted benches that were technically shaded but difficult to reach with a wheelchair, stores that allowed people to refill water bottles, and a fence that forced pedestrians to take a longer path across an exposed lot. Several marked a cool library as important even though it was outside the neighborhood boundary used in the official study.<br><br>This knowledge is sometimes called anecdotal, a word that can make it sound casual or untestable. But repeated accounts can reveal stable patterns. If many residents independently identify the same crossing as difficult, planners have evidence about a recurring experience that a passing sensor cannot measure. Residents also explain why people remain in a hot place: the bus comes infrequently, the safer route is longer, or a public building closes early.<br><br>Participatory maps have limitations of their own. Workshops may attract people who have time to attend, while night workers or residents who speak less common languages may be underrepresented. Memory can emphasize an exceptional day. A map made in August may overlook risks faced during the first sudden heat of spring.<br><br>The strongest projects therefore treat neither map as a decorative supplement to the other. Sensor data can test whether a reported hot route remains warm across many days. Resident accounts can guide sensors toward overlooked places and help planners decide which measured differences matter. Agreement between the maps increases confidence; disagreement identifies a question to investigate rather than a reason to discard one source."
    },
