    82: {
      skill_tested: "Identify why material sorting added value.",
      question: "According to the passage, why did dealers sort cloth into grades?",
      option1: "To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load",
      option2: "To ensure that pale paper products were manufactured entirely from clean white linen rather than dyed cotton",
      option3: "To conceal seams, buttons, and other fasteners that could reduce the price a mill paid for a mixed bale",
      option4: "To discourage households from saving scraps whose small quantities made warehouse storage less efficient",
      correctAnswer: "To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load",
      explanation: "Step 1: Different fibers, colors, and attachments required different processing and produced different paper.<br><br>Step 2: Grades turned a mixed load into categories whose likely yield mills could estimate.<br><br>Answer: To help mills estimate the fiber quality and processing needs of each load",
      practiceYear: 3,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage traces the changing trade in cloth rags used for papermaking.<br><br>Before paper was commonly made from wood pulp, many mills depended on worn linen and cotton. The raw material entered commerce through an untidy network. Households saved scraps, traveling collectors exchanged small goods for bundles, and dealers bought mixed loads for resale. What appeared to be refuse at one doorway could become valuable fiber several transactions later.<br><br>Demand grew as printing, record keeping, and correspondence expanded. Dealers established warehouses near transport routes and developed grades for different cloth. Clean white linen produced pale, strong paper and brought a higher price. Dyed cotton, coarse sacking, seams, and buttons required more processing or served lower grades. Sorting converted a mixed cartload into materials whose likely yield a mill could estimate.<br><br>The system depended heavily on skilled but poorly recognized labor. Sorters learned to distinguish fibers by touch and weave. They cut away fasteners and shook dust from cloth before packing it into labeled bales. Speed mattered because warehouses held large volumes, but a mistaken grade could spoil a mill batch. Dust, sharp objects hidden in bundles, and contaminated fabric made the work hazardous. Warehouses also served as financial buffers between irregular collection and continuous mill demand. A collector might arrive with only one sack, while a mill ordered bales by the wagonload. Dealers accumulated material until they could fill an order, advancing small payments long before receiving payment from the mill. They tracked weight but also cleanliness, since moisture and dirt increased shipping costs without increasing useful fiber. During busy periods, a bale's painted grade became a promise: the mill could open a sample rather than inspect every scrap. Trust in that promise allowed material gathered from hundreds of households to move as a standardized commodity. When a dealer mislabeled loads, the loss spread beyond one sale because mills began discounting every bale from that warehouse. Classification was therefore both a physical operation and a commercial reputation.<br><br>Scarcity periodically unsettled the trade. Publishers wanted more paper than collectors could supply, and sudden price increases encouraged theft from clotheslines and the sale of damp bundles made artificially heavy. Some governments restricted rag exports, hoping to reserve fiber for domestic mills. Such measures could shift routes, but they could not create cloth quickly. The industry's weakness was built into its success: rising paper demand outran a supply tied to the rate at which textiles wore out.<br><br>Mill owners experimented with straw, grasses, and other fibers. The decisive change came when mechanical and chemical processes made wood a practical mass source. Trees could be harvested specifically for paper instead of waiting for households to discard cloth. Wood pulp supported rapid expansion and reduced mills' dependence on rag markets, though it produced papers with different qualities and introduced its own environmental and industrial costs.<br><br>The rag trade did not vanish in one moment. Mills changed equipment at different rates, and some products still benefited from long textile fibers. Dealers consolidated, shifted toward other reusable materials, or supplied specialized papers. The sorting knowledge developed for cloth also influenced later recycling businesses, where separating materials by composition remains essential to value.<br><br>Histories of papermaking sometimes present wood pulp as though it simply replaced an inferior ingredient. That account misses what the rag system had accomplished. It coordinated countless small acts of saving, judging, transporting, and sorting into an industrial supply. It also transferred risk downward: households received little, collectors faced volatile prices, and sorters absorbed physical danger.<br><br>The transition is better understood as a reorganization of dependence. Wood gave mills a supply that could be expanded more directly, but it required forests, machinery, chemicals, and capital. Rags had linked paper to the afterlife of textiles; pulp linked it to a newly built extraction system. The material changed, and with it changed the people, landscapes, and costs that readers rarely saw on the finished page."
    },
