    91: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an acoustic problem.",
      question: "Why is following the loudest beat unreliable in a procession?",
      option1: "Outdoor music contains no recurring rhythm.",
      option2: "Players cannot hear nearby performers.",
      option3: "Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.",
      option4: "Concert halls amplify only footsteps.",
      correctAnswer: "Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.",
      explanation: "Step 1: Distance and reflection separate arrival time from the current beat.<br><br>Answer: Echoes and travel time can make an arriving sound late.",
      practiceYear: 3,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how musicians maintain a shared pulse while performing in a moving outdoor procession.<br><br>In a concert hall, musicians sit within a carefully arranged acoustic space. A procession removes that stability. Performers turn corners, stretch across a block, pass between reflective buildings, and encounter traffic noise. Sound takes time to travel, and echoes return from walls after the original beat. If every musician simply follows the loudest sound arriving at a given moment, the ensemble can gradually pull apart.<br><br>Procession traditions address this problem through overlapping cues. A lead drummer may mark a recurring pattern, but players also watch the motion of nearby shoulders, feet, and instrument bells. They memorize how a phrase should feel against their walking pace. Coordination is distributed across hearing, vision, movement, and expectation.<br><br>The physical line is especially important. A drummer near the rear cannot reliably synchronize with a leader half a block ahead because the leader's sound arrives late. Instead, the rear player aligns with musicians nearby, who align with those before them. This local relay introduces small delays, yet experienced groups keep them from accumulating by returning frequently to recognizable rhythmic figures.<br><br>Those figures function as checkpoints. A short phrase at the end of a musical cycle announces not merely what is being played but where the group is within the larger form. If a section has drifted, players can adjust at the checkpoint without stopping. Repetition therefore supports correction as well as audience recognition.<br><br>Street shape changes the available information. At a sharp turn, rear musicians may lose sight of the front. A leader can raise an instrument or exaggerate a step before entering the corner, sending a visible cue backward. In a narrow street, echoes may make sound less trustworthy, so bodily motion becomes more important. In an open square, visual distance increases but echoes decrease.<br><br>Experienced performers learn not to react equally to every discrepancy. An echo is delayed but predictable; a neighboring player's sudden acceleration may signal an actual tempo change or merely a stumble. Correcting too aggressively can create a new disturbance. The skill lies partly in deciding which differences contain information.<br><br>Rehearsals sometimes isolate these decisions. Directors spread musicians across a field, ask sections to play while facing away from one another, or introduce recorded street noise. The aim is not to imitate every future route. It is to make performers notice which cues remain dependable when another disappears.<br><br>Audiences participate indirectly. A dense crowd absorbs sound and narrows the pathway, while clapping can reinforce or compete with the ensemble's pulse. Performers may simplify a transition near a crowded viewing point, preserving the structure even if some ornament is lost. Such adaptation is not evidence that the music lacks a stable form. It shows that stability can depend on flexible execution.<br><br>Scholars documenting procession music therefore study more than written rhythms. Video reveals spacing and gesture; route maps identify corners and acoustic changes; interviews explain conventions that listeners might not recognize. The performed work includes a system for carrying itself through space.<br><br>A procession holds together through neither perfect simultaneity nor a single commanding signal. It survives because performers repeatedly compare partial cues, tolerate small differences, and reunite at known points. What an audience hears as one moving pulse is the result of many local acts of attention.<br><br>The same principles shape composition. Writers for moving ensembles may place especially clear rhythms before difficult corners or avoid delicate entrances where the route becomes narrow. They are not merely writing a sequence of sounds; they are anticipating how information will travel among moving bodies. The route becomes one of the conditions to which the music responds.<br><br>Recordings from a single microphone can conceal this organization. A microphone near the front captures a firm lead, while one near the rear may register echoes and delayed layers. Neither position alone represents what every performer heard. Multiple recordings reveal the work of maintaining relation across distance."
    },
