    100: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated mechanism.",
      question: "According to the passage, how does evaporation cool the clay wall?",
      option1: "Escaping water molecules carry energy away from the wall.",
      option2: "Clay converts water directly into electrical energy.",
      option3: "Warm air becomes heavier and remains near the floor.",
      option4: "Mineral deposits absorb all heat released by produce.",
      correctAnswer: "Escaping water molecules carry energy away from the wall.",
      explanation: "Step 1: Water enters pores in the clay and some of it evaporates.<br><br>Step 2: The fastest molecules escape with energy, lowering the wall's thermal energy.<br><br>Answer: Escaping water molecules carry energy away from the wall.",
      practiceYear: 3,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Sequence and Relationships",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines electricity-free cooling rooms for freshly harvested produce.<br><br>Fresh vegetables continue to respire after harvest, consuming stored sugars and releasing heat and moisture. High temperature speeds many of the reactions involved, so produce left in a warm storeroom may soften or lose water before it reaches a buyer. Mechanical refrigeration slows that process but requires dependable electricity, equipment, and maintenance.<br><br>One alternative uses walls made from porous clay. Water spread across the outer surface enters tiny channels in the clay. As some of that water evaporates into moving air, the fastest water molecules escape, carrying energy away from the wall. The inner surface cools, and air circulating through the room transfers heat to it. The system does not create cold; it moves heat from produce and interior air into water vapor outside.<br><br>Designers increase evaporation by shaping walls into thin ribs or hollow blocks. These forms expose more wet surface than a flat wall made from the same amount of clay. Openings near the floor admit relatively cool air, while vents near the roof release air warmed by produce. A shaded roof prevents direct sunlight from adding more heat than evaporation can remove. Water is supplied slowly so the wall stays damp without flooding the floor. To determine whether the clay itself is responsible for improvement, field teams compare more than one room. A useful trial may include an ordinary shaded shed, a clay room supplied with water, and an otherwise similar clay room left dry. Sensors are placed at several heights and distances from the wall, not only at the coolest location. Each room receives produce of similar type and maturity. Researchers then track mass loss, firmness, and visible spoilage as well as air temperature. These controls matter because shade alone lowers heat gain, and an empty room responds differently from one filled with respiring vegetables. A design that produces an impressive temperature reading but no reduction in crop loss may not solve the storage problem it was meant to address.<br><br>Prototype rooms have kept vegetables cooler than nearby ordinary sheds, particularly during hot, dry afternoons. The temperature difference is not constant. At night, both rooms may approach the same temperature. During humid weather, the surrounding air already contains substantial water vapor, so additional water evaporates slowly and produces less cooling. A successful result in a dry season therefore cannot be assumed to apply unchanged during a wet one.<br><br>Water use presents another constraint. Evaporative cooling may consume less energy than refrigeration, but in a dry region water itself can be scarce. Using drinking-quality water would simply exchange one resource problem for another. Some projects collect roof runoff or reuse relatively clean wash water after filtering it. Designers must also prevent mineral deposits from sealing the clay pores and must keep insects from entering vents.<br><br>Operation matters as much as construction. Packing crates tightly against a cool wall can block airflow. Leaving the door open during repeated deliveries admits warm air. Operators who record interior temperature, humidity, and water use can adjust loading schedules and identify clogged sections. A low-technology device is not a device without technique.<br><br>Larger rooms create additional difficulties. Increasing wall area can increase evaporation, but air near the center may still circulate poorly. Fans improve mixing but introduce an electricity requirement. Dividing one large room into narrower chambers may preserve passive airflow, though it uses more wall material and complicates loading. The most effective scale may therefore depend on crop volume, labor, climate, and the reliability of local resources rather than on a universal design.<br><br>Porous-clay cooling will not replace refrigeration for every crop or climate. It can, however, extend storage where moderate cooling makes the difference between a same-day sale and several days of market flexibility. Its value lies less in copying a refrigerated warehouse than in matching a clear physical mechanism to places where its requirements can actually be sustained."
    },
