    136: {
      skill_tested: "Identify the earliest indicators described in a restoration sequence.",
      question: "According to Passage A, which changes are generally monitored first after concrete channel walls are removed?",
      option1: "The return of every fish species known to have occupied the river before the concrete channel was constructed",
      option2: "Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition",
      option3: "The number and movement of visitors using the paths and overlooks added beside the restored reach",
      option4: "The height and maturity of riverside trees expected eventually to provide shade and woody debris",
      correctAnswer: "Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition",
      explanation: "Step 1: Removing the channel allows water to slow, curve, spread, and deposit sediment.<br><br>Step 2: Passage A identifies these physical changes as the first indicators often monitored.<br><br>Answer: Changes in flow, floodplain contact, and sediment deposition",
      practiceYear: 4,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Sequence and Relationships",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how to judge the restoration of an urban river.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>Removing a concrete channel from a river does not instantly restore the ecosystem that existed before construction. It changes the conditions under which recovery may occur. Water can slow along newly curved banks, spread into a floodplain, and deposit sediment in places that once received only a fast, narrow current. These physical changes are often the first indicators monitored by restoration scientists. Teams may install gauges before excavation, survey cross sections after storms, and place marked stones where sediment is expected to collect. Repeating those observations matters because a dry summer can make a redesigned channel appear stable even if it will erode during high water. Measurements taken before and after comparable flows help separate the effect of construction from ordinary seasonal variation.<br><br>Physical response, however, is not the same as biological recovery. Insects may return within a season if gravel and leaf litter accumulate. Fish that require cool pools may take longer, especially if upstream water remains warm. Riverside trees need years to produce shade and woody debris. A survey conducted shortly after construction can therefore find that the channel is behaving more naturally while the food web remains simplified.<br><br>Scientists address this difference in timing by establishing several reference points. They measure the restored reach before work begins, compare it with an unrestored reach nearby, and return during different seasons. No single indicator is decisive. Clear water may look healthy while containing few nutrients needed by small organisms; abundant algae may indicate either a productive season or excessive runoff. Evidence gains meaning through patterns among flow, temperature, sediment, plants, insects, and fish.<br><br>This approach makes patience measurable rather than vague. A project need not be declared either a triumph or a failure after one year. Managers can identify which processes have begun, which are delayed, and which remain blocked by conditions beyond the project boundary.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>Before restoration, the Millstone River ran behind warehouses and chain-link fences. Many residents knew it mainly as a blue line on municipal maps. When the concrete walls were removed, the project also added footpaths, low overlooks, and two entrances connecting the river to bus routes. Those features were not biological interventions, yet they changed what happened along the water.<br><br>A neighborhood stewardship group began weekly walks. Members recorded clogged drains and newly eroded banks, but they also counted where visitors stopped, which paths became muddy, and whether signs could be read from a wheelchair. Their observations sometimes reached maintenance crews faster than the project's annual ecological report. After a broken drain discharged cloudy water, walkers photographed the plume and traced it to a street inlet before the next scheduled sampling day.<br><br>Public access can create pressure as well as protection. A shortcut through young plants damaged one bank, and food containers accumulated near the busiest overlook. The group responded by moving a path rather than simply adding warnings. It also asked schools to stagger large visits during nesting season. In this case, use was not the opposite of restoration; it was a condition that had to be designed and revised.<br><br>Some critics describe benches, paths, and programs as amenities separate from ecological success. That distinction is useful when it prevents visitor counts from being mistaken for evidence about fish or water quality. It becomes less useful when it implies that a river can be sustained without a public able to notice and defend it. Residents do not replace laboratory analysis, but their repeated presence expands the times and places at which change can be observed.<br><br>A restored river is thus both a set of processes and a relationship. Its curves, insects, and temperatures matter whether anyone visits. Yet access can create witnesses, and witnesses can become stewards. The strongest assessment asks not whether social and ecological measures are interchangeable, but whether each helps the recovery endure."
    },
