    145: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated limitation of older stage-lighting systems.",
      question: "According to the second paragraph, one limitation of border lights was that they:",
      option1: "required actors to remain completely motionless during important speeches.",
      option2: "could illuminate a broad section of scenery instead of a single performer.",
      option3: "could be used only in theaters equipped with specialized electrical circuits.",
      option4: "produced light too weak to make painted scenery visible.",
      correctAnswer: "could illuminate a broad section of scenery instead of a single performer.",
      explanation: "<b>Step 1:</b> The second paragraph contrasts older lighting systems with the greater control offered by later equipment.<br><br><b>Step 2:</b> It specifically explains that a border light might wash an entire section of scenery rather than isolate one actor.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> could illuminate a broad section of scenery instead of a single performer.",
      practiceYear: 5,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>HUMANITIES:</b> This passage examines how changes in theater-lighting technology influenced staging practices and performance conventions.<br><br>For much of the twentieth century, changes in stage lighting were often described as if brighter lamps simply produced more visually ambitious theater. The relationship was less direct. A new lamp could exist for years before most companies could afford the fixtures, wiring, and trained crews needed to use it reliably. Even after adoption widened, directors and designers did not discard older methods at once; they often kept them because those methods created effects that newer equipment could imitate only awkwardly.<br><br>Before compact electric spotlights became common, many small theaters relied on rows of footlights, overhead border lights, and hand-operated lanterns. These systems offered limited control: a border light might wash an entire section of scenery rather than isolate one actor, while colored sheets placed in front of a lamp could fade or buckle from heat. Yet such limitations shaped recognizable practices. Actors learned to step into brighter zones for important speeches, and painted scenery was designed with broad contrasts that remained legible under an even wash. Long after more precise lamps appeared, some companies retained footlights for comic revues and period productions because the upward cast of light signaled a deliberate theatricality.<br><br>A touring troupe in the 1930s made especially inventive use of this older equipment. The company carried only six portable lanterns, too few to illuminate every part of its temporary stages at once. Instead of treating the shortage as a defect, its director arranged scenes so that performers crossed from one pool of light into another as conversations shifted. In a courtroom scene, for example, the witness stood in a narrow bright area while the lawyers remained partly dimmed; when an attorney began questioning the witness, an assistant slowly redirected one lantern toward the attorney's table. Audiences therefore experienced changes in attention as visible events. The troupe's method did not merely compensate for scarce equipment. It turned scarcity into a compositional principle.<br><br>More flexible lighting became possible through several developments that did not arrive simultaneously. Tungsten filaments made electric lamps more durable, improved reflectors concentrated light more efficiently, and adjustable shutters allowed beams to be shaped without moving an entire fixture. Dimmers also improved, though early versions were heavy and produced unwanted heat. Large urban theaters adopted these systems first because they could install new electrical circuits and maintain specialized crews. Smaller companies often rented modern fixtures only for major productions. Some touring groups even designed two versions of the same show: an elaborate lighting plan for well-equipped houses and a reduced plan for halls with fewer circuits. The difference could change where actors paused, which portions of scenery were emphasized, and how quickly scenes seemed to move. Thus a technical breakthrough could alter professional practice before it became an ordinary tool.<br><br>By midcentury, standardized plugs, lighter housings, and mass-produced control boards reduced some of those barriers. Designers could travel with a written lighting plan and expect unfamiliar theaters to reproduce at least part of it. The change affected more than convenience. A director could now build scenes around repeated transitions—a corridor narrowing into darkness, a dance floor widening into full illumination, or several isolated performers appearing in sequence—because the necessary cues could be rehearsed and repeated with greater consistency. Older broad washes did not disappear; they became one choice among many rather than the default imposed by limited equipment.<br><br>The arrangement of the control booth introduced another influence that was easy to overlook. In many theaters, the person operating lights sat far from the stage and had to respond to spoken cue numbers, musical phrases, or an actor's movement rather than to a designer's continuous instructions. As productions grew more complex, designers began marking scripts with exact cue points and numbering transitions in advance. This practical separation between designer and operator encouraged a convention that still structures rehearsal: lighting changes are treated as discrete, repeatable events that can be called, recorded, and revised. The convention arose not from brighter lamps alone but from the interaction of equipment, cost, inherited stage habits, and the physical organization of theatrical work."
    },
