    163: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated reason for a character's preference.",
      question: "According to Passage B, why did the narrator originally prefer working after visitors had left?",
      option1: "The telescope produced clearer images only after public hours.",
      option2: "The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.",
      option3: "Arun prohibited volunteers from speaking with visitors.",
      option4: "The observatory recorded temperatures only when the dome was empty.",
      correctAnswer: "The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.",
      explanation: "Step 1: The opening of Passage B contrasts orderly columns of measurements with crowds that interrupt and ask questions.<br><br>Step 2: This contrast directly explains why the narrator favors the quiet work performed after visitors leave.<br><br>Answer: The narrator was more comfortable with measurements than with crowds.",
      practiceYear: 5,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages present narrators whose usually private work unexpectedly becomes public.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>For four months I had cleaned a storm-darkened mural in the city museum one square inch at a time. Most mornings, before visitors arrived, I climbed the scaffold with cotton swabs, solvent, and a lamp angled close to the wall. Beneath a gray film, a harbor slowly returned: copper roofs, green water, and workers unloading baskets of silver fish. The work rewarded patience so quietly that I could leave each evening without anyone noticing what had changed.<br><br>On the day the scaffold came down, the museum director, Elise, asked me to speak at the mural's unveiling. I told her the painting should be the visible thing. Conservators, I said, were successful when no one could detect where their hands had been. Elise accepted my refusal, though the pause before she answered suggested that she considered invisibility an incomplete ambition.<br><br>During the unveiling, I stood behind the last row of folding chairs. A reporter praised the mural's newly brilliant colors and asked Elise whether modern cleaning chemicals had made the restoration simple. Elise could have offered the pleasing answer. Instead, she described the tests performed along the frame, the weeks spent distinguishing soot from original shadow, and the afternoon when I stopped the cleaning because a blue pigment had begun reacting unpredictably. She did not call me to the front, but she said my name.<br><br>Several people turned. Heat rose beneath my collar. Yet the attention did not feel like applause. It felt like the moment when I switched on my close lamp and made a previously hidden decision available for inspection. A conservator's restraint, I realized, could disappear as completely as careless work if no one explained it. I remained behind the chairs, but when the reporter approached afterward, I showed her the test photographs and told her why one strip of harbor sky remained slightly dim.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>My favorite hour at the Hillcrest Observatory began after the public left. I would cap the visitor telescope, enter its temperature readings, and help Arun compare the night's observations with older logs. I had joined the volunteer team because numbers behaved better than crowds. A column of measurements never interrupted, leaned too close, or asked whether a faint smudge was really a galaxy.<br><br>Then a comet appeared earlier in the season than predicted. The observatory announced an additional viewing night, and two hundred people climbed the hill. Arun placed me beside the main telescope with instructions to explain what visitors were seeing. I reminded him that I maintained equipment; I did not perform. He handed me a red flashlight and replied that knowing how the instrument behaved was precisely why I should speak.<br><br>My first explanations were mechanical. I named the lens, stated the comet's estimated distance, and warned everyone not to touch the focus knob. A boy at the eyepiece looked for several seconds and then announced that he saw only a fuzzy seed. The waiting adults laughed, but the comparison was accurate. I asked him what might happen if the seed had a tail made of dust. He described wind carrying it through darkness. Using his image, I explained how sunlight pushed material away from the comet.<br><br>After that, the line became less like an audience and more like a problem we were solving together. One visitor noticed that the comet seemed still while thin clouds moved. Another asked why the tail did not trail behind it like a bicycle ribbon. Their questions forced me to translate measurements I had understood only in the observatory's shorthand. Arun stayed near the steps, directing newcomers toward me but never answering in my place.<br><br>When the final visitor left, I expected relief. Instead, the silent dome seemed briefly unfinished. Recording the temperature still mattered, as did protecting the focus mechanism from impatient fingers. But the observations in our logs had acquired voices: a seed, a ribbon, moving clouds. I had not surrendered the private precision I valued. I had discovered that explaining it could reveal portions of the work that even I had not fully seen."
    },
