    172: {
      skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated benefit of linked storage sites.",
      question: "According to the second paragraph, why are orders from several farms combined at a hub?",
      option1: "To eliminate labels before delivery",
      option2: "To create loads large enough for economical transport",
      option3: "To require every farm to grow the same crop",
      option4: "To keep all produce at one temperature",
      correctAnswer: "To create loads large enough for economical transport",
      explanation: "Step 1: Farms bring produce to nearby cooling sites.<br><br>Step 2: The hub combines smaller orders into loads that can be transported economically.<br><br>Answer: To create loads large enough for economical transport",
      practiceYear: 5,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines a cooperative system for sharing agricultural cold storage and transport.<br><br>A small farm may harvest enough produce to supply several markets yet too little to justify owning a refrigerated warehouse and truck. Without cooling, leafy crops lose quality quickly, and a delayed pickup can erase the value of a week's work. In some regions, farms address this mismatch through cooperatives that pool storage rooms, vehicles, and schedules while leaving production decisions with individual members.<br><br>The simplest version is a shared building. Farmers reserve space, deliver labeled crates, and pay according to use. More developed systems link several small cooling sites with one larger hub. A farm can bring produce to the nearest site, where orders from multiple farms are combined into loads large enough for economical transport. The hub may also sort orders by destination, temperature requirement, or delivery day.<br><br>The network works because members need different amounts of capacity at different times. One farm's berry harvest peaks before another farm's late vegetables. Empty space can move, in an accounting sense, from one member to another without any wall changing position. Coordinators track reservations and actual use so that a farm expecting a large harvest does not block space it ultimately leaves empty.<br><br>Flexibility becomes especially valuable when demand changes suddenly. If a school cafeteria postpones an order, its crates can remain cool while a restaurant order leaves as scheduled. If one vehicle requires repair, loads can be reassigned among other routes. Such adjustments depend on accurate labels and prompt updates. A crate listed under the wrong destination occupies not only physical space but also a false position in the schedule.<br><br>The central hub produces efficiencies, but it can also concentrate risk. A power failure there may affect produce from many farms at once. Cooperatives reduce that vulnerability with backup generators, temperature alarms, agreements with nearby warehouses, and rules for deciding which goods should move first. Some maintain limited cross-connections among smaller sites so that not every emergency must pass through the hub.<br><br>Access to the network is not automatically equal. Farms close to a cooling site spend less time and fuel delivering produce. Larger members may have staff available to update forecasts, while a grower working alone may confirm a harvest only after picking begins. If reservation penalties are too strict, the system can punish the members with the least predictable labor and crops. If rules are too loose, unused reservations can deprive others of space.<br><br>Several cooperatives address this tension by dividing capacity into layers. Members reserve a dependable base amount in advance, while a smaller flexible pool is allocated closer to harvest. Coordinators may also hold emergency space for unexpected heat or vehicle failure. The layers do not guarantee that every request will be met. They prevent the entire system from being governed either by rigid forecasts or by last-minute competition.<br><br>Shared infrastructure can change what farms choose to grow, but that effect should not be confused with direct control. Reliable cooling may make a delicate crop less risky or allow harvest to occur at a better stage of ripeness. Each farm still weighs soil, labor, market, and experience. The cooperative expands feasible choices; it does not make those choices identical.<br><br>Evaluating the network therefore requires more than counting crates at the hub. Useful measures include spoilage avoided, travel distance, unused reservations, emergency transfers, delivery reliability, and which members gain access during peak weeks. A highly efficient route may still serve only the largest farms. A lightly used rural site may appear inefficient while providing the only practical entry point for distant members.<br><br>The cooperative's strength lies in coordinated difference. Farms do not need identical crops or schedules for the network to work; their differences can help smooth demand. But coordination makes dependencies visible. Cooling, transport, information, and fair rules must reinforce one another. The system succeeds not when every crate passes through the largest building, but when produce can move through several linked options without one member's uncertainty becoming everyone else's loss."
    },
