    199: {
      skill_tested: "Identify a stated contrast in characters' work methods.",
      question: "According to the third paragraph, how do Julian's and Mara's approaches differ?",
      option1: "Julian examines every paper for personal history, while Mara tests equipment and records its serial numbers for sale.",
      option2: "Julian plans to preserve everything associated with the cinema.",
      option3: "Julian limits his work to the projection booth, while Mara refuses to help sort objects found in the lobby.",
      option4: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.",
      correctAnswer: "Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.",
      explanation: "Step 1: Julian tests, records, and photographs equipment.<br><br>Step 2: Mara opens drawers and attends to found objects and unsent notes.<br><br>Answer: Julian documents equipment for sale, while Mara examines objects and papers for personal meaning.",
      practiceYear: 6,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Character and Relationship Analysis",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>LITERARY NARRATIVE:</b> This passage describes two siblings clearing their late father's neighborhood cinema.<br><br>Mara arrived at the Rialto with boxes folded flat beneath her arm. Julian was already inside, standing on the concession counter to remove the hand-painted price board. He had labeled three piles—KEEP, SELL, DISCARD—in chalk on the lobby floor. Mara set her boxes directly across the lines. Julian looked down at them, then at her, and climbed from the counter without saying hello.<br><br>They had not worked together since they were teenagers, when their father assigned Mara the ticket window and Julian the projectors. Mara remembered Julian emerging from the booth only between reels, smelling of warm dust. Julian remembered Mara letting neighborhood children enter with half-price tickets when their father was not watching. Each had privately considered the other's job the easier one.<br><br>Now the cinema had been closed for seven months. A buyer wanted the building empty by Friday. Julian moved quickly, testing equipment, checking serial numbers, and photographing anything that might be sold. Mara opened drawers. She found birthday candles, umbrellas, a single silver shoe, and envelopes containing notes their father had never mailed. Julian called these discoveries delays. Mara called his photographs another way of refusing to look.<br><br>The argument began over a dented metal film can. Julian placed it in DISCARD. Mara retrieved it, recognizing the faded title of the first movie their father had shown after buying the Rialto. The can was empty, but she insisted it belonged in KEEP. Julian said that if memory were enough, they would have to preserve every broken chair. Mara accused him of treating the cinema like a failed machine.<br><br>Julian carried the can upstairs to the projection booth. Mara followed, prepared to continue. Instead, he opened a cabinet containing rows of notebooks. For every screening, their father had recorded attendance, bulb hours, sound problems, and complaints. In the margins Julian had added instructions in his own narrow handwriting. He explained that during their father's last year, he had returned each Sunday to inspect the projectors, though the two of them had barely spoken. The photographs downstairs were for an equipment archive he intended to give the local film club.<br><br>Mara sat in the operator's chair. She had interpreted Julian's speed as indifference because it resembled his silence, and she had always treated silence as empty. The notebooks made that interpretation harder. Julian, meanwhile, turned the film can over and discovered a date scratched into its base. It was not the opening-night can Mara remembered; it came from a repair made years later. Her certainty had supplied a history the object did not possess.<br><br>They returned to the lobby and erased the three chalk labels. In their place they made four: SELL, RECYCLE, CLUB ARCHIVE, FAMILY. The empty can went into FAMILY—not because Mara's story was accurate, Julian said, but because the argument now belonged to them. Mara placed the price board in CLUB ARCHIVE after Julian showed her the pencil calculations on its back.<br><br>By evening, their methods had not merged. Julian still photographed each projector part before packing it, and Mara still read every scrap of paper. But he began bringing her objects whose histories he could not identify, while she stopped assuming that obscurity made an object precious. They worked at different speeds, crossing the lobby with fewer collisions.<br><br>Neither apologized directly, but each had begun asking questions where accusation had previously supplied an answer.<br><br>Before leaving, Julian switched on the auditorium aisle lights. Most bulbs had failed, producing a broken path toward the screen. Mara resisted the urge to turn the pattern into a symbol. She simply stood beside him while the remaining lights warmed. On Friday, the building would be empty. Nothing they sorted could prevent that. Still, the four labeled areas behind them held a record neither would have made alone: not the cinema preserved, but two incomplete ways of remembering made answerable to each other."
    },
