    226: {
      skill_tested: "Identify a stated reason for repeating measurements.",
      question: "According to the third paragraph, why are mobile survey routes repeated at similar times?",
      option1: "To prevent fixed sensors from recording regional weather",
      option2: "To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions",
      option3: "To ensure that every route passes through an irrigated field",
      option4: "To make all surface materials reach the same temperature",
      correctAnswer: "To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions",
      explanation: "Step 1: Heat differences among locations change during the day.<br><br>Step 2: A single route may also capture a cloud, traffic delay, or sprinkler, so repetition helps identify patterns that persist beyond those temporary events.<br><br>Answer: To distinguish durable location patterns from temporary conditions",
      practiceYear: 7,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>SOCIAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how researchers measure heat across urban neighborhoods.<br><br>During an evening survey, a volunteer carrying a temperature sensor turned from a tree-lined street into a broad commercial parking lot. The reading climbed before she had crossed half the pavement. Several blocks later, beside an irrigated playing field, it fell again. The official weather station for the city reported one temperature throughout this walk. That station was not malfunctioning. It was answering a different question.<br><br>Weather stations are deliberately standardized. Their instruments are shielded from direct sunlight, positioned above suitable ground, and kept away from nearby surfaces that could distort regional measurements. This consistency makes observations from different days and cities comparable. But a station designed to represent general conditions cannot describe every place where residents encounter heat. A person waiting beside a dark wall, walking beneath mature trees, or sleeping in an upper apartment experiences conditions shaped by a much smaller area.<br><br>Researchers seeking that local variation use mobile sensors on cars, bicycles, or backpacks, as well as fixed sensors distributed among neighborhoods. Routes are repeated at similar times because the temperature gap between locations changes during the day. Sunlit pavement may be hottest in late afternoon, while brick and concrete can continue releasing stored heat after sunset. A route measured only once might capture a passing cloud, traffic delay, or sprinkler rather than a durable pattern.<br><br>Surface materials explain part of the variation. Dark roofs and pavement absorb solar energy; plants shade surfaces and release water vapor; closely spaced buildings can restrict air movement. Yet the effect of any feature depends on its surroundings. A small tree may shade one bench without changing the temperature of a wide intersection. A park can cool adjacent blocks under some wind conditions but have little influence farther away. Researchers therefore examine combinations of cover, building form, moisture, and airflow rather than assigning one fixed cooling value to every tree or lawn.<br><br>Distributed measurements have often revealed that nighttime differences deserve as much attention as daytime peaks. Neighborhoods with abundant masonry and little vegetation may release stored heat slowly, giving buildings and bodies less opportunity to cool. Two areas that reach similar afternoon temperatures can therefore present different overnight risks. This distinction matters because health depends not only on the highest reading but also on the duration of exposure and the possibility of recovery.<br><br>Local data, however, can create an illusion of precision. A sensor attached too close to a vehicle may record engine heat. Residents who volunteer equipment may be concentrated in neighborhoods already active in environmental programs, leaving other areas sparsely measured. Even a correctly placed sensor describes its immediate surroundings, not every apartment on the block. Researchers compare instruments, document placement, repeat routes, and report gaps so that a detailed map does not imply certainty it has not earned.<br><br>The maps become most useful when combined with information about people and buildings. A hot block with air-conditioned offices may require a different response from a slightly cooler block where many residents lack reliable cooling. Tree planting may help one street, while shaded transit stops, reflective roofs, adjusted work hours, or accessible cooling centers may address another form of exposure sooner. Temperature data identify physical patterns; they do not select a policy by themselves.<br><br>This is why neighborhood measurement supplements rather than replaces the official station. The station provides a stable regional record used for forecasts and long-term comparison. Distributed sensors reveal variation inside that regional condition and help explain why one citywide warning can have unequal consequences. The apparent conflict between one official number and many local numbers disappears once their scales are understood.<br><br>The volunteer's rising and falling readings were therefore neither a correction of the official temperature nor a collection of isolated curiosities. Repeated carefully, such walks can show where heat is stored, where it is released, and where people receive little relief. The broader lesson is methodological: before treating a measurement as incomplete, researchers must ask which scale it was designed to represent—and before treating a detailed map as complete, they must ask what it still fails to observe."
    },
