    244: {
      skill_tested: "Identify a tradeoff.",
      question: "What risk accompanies waiting too long at a stopover?",
      option1: "The bird may lose favorable winds or arrive late for nesting space.",
      option2: "Its wing length immediately decreases.",
      option3: "It can no longer measure wind direction.",
      option4: "All food becomes permanently unavailable after one night.",
      correctAnswer: "The bird may lose favorable winds or arrive late for nesting space.",
      explanation: "Step 1: The opening contrasts fuel gain with timing costs.<br><br>Answer: The bird may lose favorable winds or arrive late for nesting space.",
      practiceYear: 7,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>NATURAL SCIENCE:</b> This passage examines how migratory shorebirds decide when to leave a coastal stopover.<br><br>Migration maps often show clean arrows between breeding and wintering grounds. For an individual bird, however, the journey is a sequence of uncertain departures. At a stopover, the bird must rebuild energy reserves while time, weather, predators, and competition continue to change. Leaving too soon may mean running out of fuel. Waiting too long may forfeit favorable winds or delay arrival at a destination where nesting space is limited.<br><br>Researchers studying sandpipers along a tidal bay weigh and tag birds during brief captures. Small radio transmitters record when each bird departs. Because mass alone does not reveal fat reserves precisely, researchers also measure wing length and use repeated samples to estimate a fuel index. Weather stations record wind direction, speed, pressure, and cloud cover.<br><br>A simple hypothesis predicts that birds leave upon reaching a fixed fuel threshold. Fuel matters strongly: very lean birds rarely depart on long flights. Yet tagged birds above the apparent threshold sometimes remain for several nights, while moderately fueled birds occasionally leave. The exceptions suggest that readiness is conditional rather than a single number.<br><br>Wind provides one condition. A tailwind reduces the energy needed to cover a given distance, whereas a headwind increases it. On nights with strong supportive winds, birds with moderate reserves depart more often. Well-fueled birds sometimes leave in weaker winds, apparently able to pay the added energetic cost. Fuel and weather can therefore substitute for each other within limits.<br><br>Season changes those limits. Early in migration, a bird may wait for better conditions because several stopping opportunities remain and arrival is not yet urgent. Later, prolonged waiting may be costly. Near the end of the migration window, departures occur under a wider range of winds. The same headwind that discourages travel in one week may be accepted in another.<br><br>Researchers tested whether birds respond directly to weather or merely follow a daily schedule. They compared individuals captured at similar dates and fuel levels but exposed to different wind changes after sunset. Departure probability rose within hours when wind shifted to a supportive direction. This rapid response was inconsistent with a schedule based only on date.<br><br>Habitat quality also affects the decision indirectly. In areas where food is abundant, a bird can gain fuel rapidly and may benefit from one additional feeding cycle. Where prey has been depleted, waiting produces little gain. Birds at poor sites may depart with lower reserves, perhaps aiming for a nearby stop rather than the full distance expected from birds at richer sites.<br><br>Predation risk complicates observation. A falcon passing over the mudflat can suppress feeding, lowering the day's fuel gain. It may also encourage birds to leave the dangerous site. Researchers cannot infer which effect dominates from departure time alone; they combine feeding observations, mass change, and flight direction.<br><br>No tag reports the bird's intention. Statistical models estimate how strongly each measured factor changes departure probability, but unmeasured conditions remain. Age, prior route experience, health, and conditions beyond the bay may all contribute. A model that predicts many departures correctly is still a description of tendencies, not a command followed by every bird.<br><br>The decision to depart is best understood as a moving threshold. Fuel establishes what is physically possible, while wind, season, habitat, and danger alter what is worthwhile now. The arrow on a migration map is therefore the accumulated result of many moments in which waiting and leaving exchange advantages.<br><br>Researchers improve their models by testing predictions at another bay rather than merely refining the first dataset. A relationship that appears strong at a sheltered site may weaken where storms develop faster or feeding flats vanish earlier with the tide. Successful prediction across locations suggests a general process; failure can reveal a condition the original study overlooked.<br><br>Departure is not the same as successful arrival. Some transmitters detect a bird leaving but not where it lands. Linking receiver arrays along the route helps distinguish a long migratory flight from a short relocation to another feeding area. That distinction prevents researchers from treating every nighttime movement as equivalent."
    },
