    280: {
      skill_tested: "Identify a detail that changes the meaning of a record.",
      question: "In Passage A, what does the replacement of a crossed-out name with Rosa indicate?",
      option1: "Rosa owned Maple Lanes.",
      option2: "The league prohibited factory employees from bowling.",
      option3: "The score sheet belonged to a children's league.",
      option4: "Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.",
      correctAnswer: "Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.",
      explanation: "Step 1: The sheet replaces another player's name with Rosa's.<br><br>Step 2: Dad explains that she filled in when machine breakdowns kept the regular team at work.<br><br>Answer: Rosa substituted when the regular team was delayed at work.",
      practiceYear: 8,
      difficulty: "Medium",
      module: "Textual Details",
      imageSources: [],
      passage: "<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages describe two narrators reconsidering ordinary community records.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>When Maple Lanes closed, my father asked me to clear the cabinet beneath Lane 6. I expected spare parts. Instead, I found bundles of paper score sheets tied with shoe laces. Most showed unimpressive games: 112, 87, 134. I carried the first bundle toward recycling.<br><br>Dad stopped me because he recognized the handwriting on top. The sheet came from the Tuesday factory league, whose members bowled after the late shift. Beside one frame, someone had written missed bus; another player's name was crossed out and replaced by Rosa. Dad explained that Rosa had filled in whenever a machine breakdown kept the usual team at work.<br><br>We spread the sheets across two lanes. Their numbers mattered, but not chiefly as records of excellence. Repeated names showed who returned year after year. Team titles changed when factories merged. Children added drawings while waiting for parents. One winter's sheets ended early each week because heating fuel was rationed. The papers held a calendar of ordinary participation. Dates on the backs showed that sheets had been reused as waiting lists and snack-bar inventories before entering the cabinet. Pinholes matched the bulletin board by the entrance. A coffee ring covered one winning score but left the penciled team roster readable. Their survival was accidental, yet the layers of reuse helped explain how thoroughly the alley belonged to its regulars.<br><br>I did not save every sheet. Water had fused some into unreadable blocks, and many duplicated information already present elsewhere. I selected examples from different leagues and years, then invited former bowlers to annotate copies. Their memories occasionally conflicted. We kept the disagreement in the catalog rather than forcing the cleaner story.<br><br>Before the cabinet, I had thought an archive began when someone intentionally preserved important events. Maple Lanes taught me that it can also begin when routine records outlive the routine that made them seem disposable.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>For thirty-eight years, Mr. Adeyemi read the rain gauge behind the volunteer fire station at seven each morning. After he retired, the town library received his ruled notebooks. I admired their persistence but doubted their scientific value. Several entries were smudged, and on holidays the usual morning observation sometimes appeared at noon.<br><br>A regional climatologist examined the notebooks before I finished arranging them. She agreed that they could not simply be combined with automated station data. Observation time affects daily totals, gauges differ, and a wind-driven storm may deposit unevenly. Yet she did not dismiss the record. She asked whether Mr. Adeyemi had noted equipment changes and whether nearby stations could help identify unusual gaps.<br><br>His margins answered more than I expected. Gauge moved away from new roof, one entry read. Another noted that a reading followed two days when the road was blocked. Newspaper clippings documented hail, while a seed-store calendar marked the first delivery delayed by muddy fields. These materials did not transform every number into certainty, but they made many numbers interpretable.<br><br>Former firefighters added another layer. They remembered using the notebook to decide when brush-fire warnings should be posted and when the training field was too saturated for trucks. To them, the record was not merely an imperfect series awaiting professional rescue. It had already guided local decisions, even as its scientific reuse required caution.<br><br>We digitized the readings with flags for changed equipment, uncertain times, and missing days. The climatologist later used only part of the series in a regional analysis. At first I felt protective of the excluded pages. Then I realized that usefulness did not require every entry to serve every purpose.<br><br>The notebooks now sit beside the fire station's annual reports. They are neither flawless climate data nor simple memorabilia. They show a disciplined practice, its interruptions, and the decisions built around it. Preserving them means retaining both the measurements and the limits that allow future readers to use those measurements honestly."
    },
